WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:09.585
&gt;&gt; Okay. It's my honor today
to introduce Susan Athey.

00:00:09.586 --> 00:00:12.050
She's the Economics of Technology

00:00:12.051 --> 00:00:15.185
Professor at the Stanford
Graduate School of Business.

00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:17.640
Susan received her
bachelor's degree

00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:19.260
from Duke and her
PhD at Stanford,

00:00:19.261 --> 00:00:21.015
and not to be outdone by that,

00:00:21.016 --> 00:00:25.840
Duke gave her an honorary
PhD degree, or a doctorate.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.860
Susan's research focuses
on the intersection

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:30.640
of machine learning
and econometrics,

00:00:30.641 --> 00:00:34.825
marketplace design, and the
economics of digitization.

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:36.370
She advices governments and

00:00:36.371 --> 00:00:41.820
businesses on marketplace design
and platform economics.

00:00:41.821 --> 00:00:44.040
I remember when we

00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:46.850
first heard of her and met
her when she came in as,

00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:51.675
in 2007 or so, as
Microsoft's Chief Economist,

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:54.450
you call it Consulting
Chief Economist.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.980
As a preview of the forthcoming
fireside chat which

00:01:00.981 --> 00:01:03.455
was this time today
featured early,

00:01:03.456 --> 00:01:08.185
filmed earlier than
the distinguished lecture,

00:01:08.186 --> 00:01:11.330
Susan mentioned that
Steve Balm at the time said,

00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:12.725
he was just having,

00:01:12.726 --> 00:01:13.970
was interested in
learning more about

00:01:13.971 --> 00:01:16.100
this online stuff
and you'll hear more

00:01:16.101 --> 00:01:19.380
about that when you
see the fireside chat.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.148
Susan was elected to

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:23.590
the National Academy
of Science in 2012,

00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:26.300
and the American Academy of
Arts and Sciences in 2008.

00:01:26.301 --> 00:01:28.290
She's taught in
the economics departments

00:01:28.291 --> 00:01:30.610
at MIT, Stanford, and Harvard.

00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:34.660
In 2007, Susan was

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:38.170
awarded the James John
Bates Clark Medal.

00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:42.640
This is known as
the Nobel Prize for

00:01:42.641 --> 00:01:48.460
younger economists and in
this case under 40 and yeah,

00:01:48.461 --> 00:01:50.390
I guess you're still
under 40, which is great.

00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:53.900
&gt;&gt; Exactly, [inaudible]

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:55.810
&gt;&gt; Today, she'll be speaking
about large-scale models of

00:01:55.811 --> 00:01:57.830
consumer choice and
counterfactual inference.

00:01:57.831 --> 00:02:00.430
A really interesting
area and with

00:02:00.431 --> 00:02:03.170
implications not
just for services,

00:02:03.171 --> 00:02:07.930
but for core econometric models

00:02:07.931 --> 00:02:10.720
and even Biology I
think so. Take it away.

00:02:10.721 --> 00:02:13.730
&gt;&gt; Great. Thanks so much.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.880
So, I'm delighted to be here at

00:02:20.881 --> 00:02:24.080
MSR and really it's
wonderful to be back

00:02:24.081 --> 00:02:27.095
and see old friends and
particularly people

00:02:27.096 --> 00:02:30.875
who really introduced me to
this whole research agenda.

00:02:30.876 --> 00:02:35.920
So, my relationship with
Microsoft started in 2007,

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:38.510
and I was brought in at
that point because I knew

00:02:38.511 --> 00:02:41.240
things about marketplace
design and auctions,

00:02:41.241 --> 00:02:43.610
and Microsoft realized that they

00:02:43.611 --> 00:02:45.960
just built up the search engine
and they were trying,

00:02:45.961 --> 00:02:47.390
they really hadn't started

00:02:47.391 --> 00:02:50.630
optimizing the ad platform
for the search engine at all,

00:02:50.631 --> 00:02:52.460
and they also had a lot
of strategic questions

00:02:52.461 --> 00:02:54.670
about how to compete
with Google and so on.

00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:57.080
So, I came in but I
had no experience

00:02:57.081 --> 00:03:00.050
with working with companies,
or with engineering.

00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:01.910
I didn't know what
machine learning

00:03:01.911 --> 00:03:05.000
meant and I had never worked
with large-scale systems.

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:07.080
In some sense I was a baby
along with Bing Ads,

00:03:07.081 --> 00:03:09.080
because at the time
I came in in order

00:03:09.081 --> 00:03:11.220
to analyze the data
from Bing Ads,

00:03:11.221 --> 00:03:12.935
which at that point
we called adCenter,

00:03:12.936 --> 00:03:15.180
you actually had to take up a PC,

00:03:15.181 --> 00:03:19.170
there is a woman named
Triza Nong and she had to take

00:03:19.171 --> 00:03:20.510
a PC and spend

00:03:20.511 --> 00:03:23.390
the entire weekend copying
the files onto a hard drive,

00:03:23.391 --> 00:03:25.050
which they then FedExed to me

00:03:25.051 --> 00:03:29.940
in New England and then
I worked with that data.

00:03:29.941 --> 00:03:31.220
So, I actually provided some of

00:03:31.221 --> 00:03:33.110
the first insights about Bing Ads

00:03:33.111 --> 00:03:34.610
because I was the first one
who could actually

00:03:34.611 --> 00:03:36.500
break the data into deciles,

00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:37.760
or centiles, because actually

00:03:37.761 --> 00:03:39.140
the computing
infrastructure didn't

00:03:39.141 --> 00:03:42.660
exist there to actually do that.

00:03:42.661 --> 00:03:45.150
So, we grew up

00:03:45.151 --> 00:03:47.265
alongside the building,
the infrastructure,

00:03:47.266 --> 00:03:50.060
to optimize the system and it was

00:03:50.061 --> 00:03:52.910
really exciting to be
there in those early days,

00:03:52.911 --> 00:03:56.280
when everything was
new and every insight

00:03:56.281 --> 00:03:58.320
you brought from
data could really

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.640
be put into great action.

00:04:00.641 --> 00:04:04.010
So, in those early days,

00:04:04.011 --> 00:04:07.250
I was thinking that what
I could bring to bear

00:04:07.251 --> 00:04:10.940
was things about economics
and advertiser behavior,

00:04:10.941 --> 00:04:12.800
because a lot of the engineers
who had brought them

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.945
brought in came from other
products and Microsoft.

00:04:14.946 --> 00:04:17.390
So, there was
this big order database

00:04:17.391 --> 00:04:20.560
where people put their bids
for search auctions,

00:04:20.561 --> 00:04:24.080
but there was no real reason

00:04:24.081 --> 00:04:27.345
you had to think about those
as active participants,

00:04:27.346 --> 00:04:29.555
because the database
was just sitting there.

00:04:29.556 --> 00:04:31.050
So, it's easy to obstruct

00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:32.870
away from you and the idea
that you had customers.

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:35.965
So, I was trying to bring
in economic ideas there.

00:04:35.966 --> 00:04:37.880
I would say things
like, ''Hey guys,

00:04:37.881 --> 00:04:40.490
if we reduce
the quality of the ads,

00:04:40.491 --> 00:04:41.900
or if we raise the prices,

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:44.330
the advertisers might
not buy as much."

00:04:44.331 --> 00:04:46.290
There would be a little bit
of a pushback,

00:04:46.291 --> 00:04:49.030
like, well where's
the evidence for that?

00:04:49.031 --> 00:04:50.870
Actually it turned out, in

00:04:50.871 --> 00:04:52.430
some sense it's not
true because if

00:04:52.431 --> 00:04:56.090
the order database is
fixed and you query it,

00:04:56.091 --> 00:04:59.240
the order database won't
change instantaneously.

00:04:59.241 --> 00:05:00.590
So, you had to think about

00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:02.090
different forms of
analytics as well as

00:05:02.091 --> 00:05:03.350
different ways to experiment to

00:05:03.351 --> 00:05:05.075
really get it that
advertiser behavior.

00:05:05.076 --> 00:05:07.085
So, as I started trying
to dig into this,

00:05:07.086 --> 00:05:09.770
I started realizing
that although I had

00:05:09.771 --> 00:05:12.890
great insights about how to do

00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:16.215
empirical analysis on
small data sets from economics,

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:18.800
there was so much that I really

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:21.745
was not prepared to do with
these larger datasets.

00:05:21.746 --> 00:05:24.890
Being one economist in a team of,

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.150
all the engineers were coming

00:05:26.151 --> 00:05:27.870
from engineering disciplines,

00:05:27.871 --> 00:05:30.590
and I saw people who I was
able to collaborate with,

00:05:30.591 --> 00:05:33.455
all coming from computer
science disciplines.

00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:35.930
In order to do
these empirical projects

00:05:35.931 --> 00:05:38.130
I really needed to
become bilingual.

00:05:38.131 --> 00:05:40.579
So, that was
an amazing experience

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.110
for me and actually,
Patrice is here,

00:05:42.111 --> 00:05:44.435
we had these early
arguments where

00:05:44.436 --> 00:05:47.195
we really we're clashing,

00:05:47.196 --> 00:05:48.890
and there's a sense of
like, I'm an economist,

00:05:48.891 --> 00:05:51.005
I'm like I've been doing
empirical work my whole life,

00:05:51.006 --> 00:05:53.360
of course I know how to do this.

00:05:53.361 --> 00:05:54.680
Patrice like, well,
we've been doing

00:05:54.681 --> 00:05:56.510
machine learning, we
know how to do this.

00:05:56.511 --> 00:06:00.080
The conversations where I

00:06:00.081 --> 00:06:04.085
figured out where we
were miscommunicating,

00:06:04.086 --> 00:06:05.570
where I wasn't understanding

00:06:05.571 --> 00:06:07.280
where other people
were coming from,

00:06:07.281 --> 00:06:08.660
were really enlightening for me.

00:06:08.661 --> 00:06:10.490
I actually, I still think about

00:06:10.491 --> 00:06:12.350
Patrice all the time
because when I go out

00:06:12.351 --> 00:06:16.940
and give seminars to
machine learning crowds,

00:06:16.941 --> 00:06:20.200
I harken back to
the way that I first

00:06:20.201 --> 00:06:22.535
crystallized how to explain

00:06:22.536 --> 00:06:24.840
why what I was doing
or think that way,

00:06:24.841 --> 00:06:26.390
I was thinking that was
a little bit different.

00:06:26.391 --> 00:06:28.400
Then at the same time,

00:06:28.401 --> 00:06:31.550
I really shifted
my career a lot in

00:06:31.551 --> 00:06:34.005
the last six or seven years to

00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:36.700
try to bring machine
learning into economics.

00:06:36.701 --> 00:06:38.330
So, I feel like there's actually

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:43.285
this really exciting marrying
of two disciplines.

00:06:43.286 --> 00:06:45.670
Both its transformative
to economics but

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.350
also there were a few things
that we did know

00:06:48.351 --> 00:06:50.720
along the way that were useful

00:06:50.721 --> 00:06:53.880
and can also be embedded
in machine learning.

00:06:53.881 --> 00:06:55.970
Now, there's some of you
here to which this is

00:06:55.971 --> 00:06:59.060
not new because one of
the few places that

00:06:59.061 --> 00:07:01.505
really has a great
concentration of people

00:07:01.506 --> 00:07:03.140
bringing together
machine-learning and

00:07:03.141 --> 00:07:05.130
causal inference is Microsoft.

00:07:05.131 --> 00:07:08.700
We have it among some of the
economists from Greg Louis,

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:10.830
in New England and Matt Tatty,

00:07:10.831 --> 00:07:13.005
work that he did while he
was here on project Alice.

00:07:13.006 --> 00:07:14.640
Of course [inaudible] is here

00:07:14.641 --> 00:07:16.940
and we've had people come
through like Leon Batu,

00:07:16.941 --> 00:07:18.800
who is someone that we
thought a lot about

00:07:18.801 --> 00:07:22.040
counterfactual reasoning and
adCenter with and so on.

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:24.740
So, and of course a lot

00:07:24.741 --> 00:07:27.290
of the work in the New York
Lab on contextual bandits

00:07:27.291 --> 00:07:33.295
also is very solidly in
the causal inference framework.

00:07:33.296 --> 00:07:36.275
So, when I generalize

00:07:36.276 --> 00:07:37.670
about machine learning and if

00:07:37.671 --> 00:07:39.325
I slip anywhere along the way,

00:07:39.326 --> 00:07:41.305
just let me make
sure to start with,

00:07:41.306 --> 00:07:44.185
I'm carving out that elite.

00:07:44.186 --> 00:07:47.135
Brilliant group of exceptions

00:07:47.136 --> 00:07:50.055
that is bringing
these things together.

00:07:50.056 --> 00:07:52.940
When I'm generalizing, I'm
thinking more about like

00:07:52.941 --> 00:07:57.400
the broad world of image
recognition with neural nets.

00:07:57.401 --> 00:07:58.985
So, just to not offend anyone.

00:07:58.986 --> 00:08:00.745
I realized that not
everybody doing

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:02.890
machine learning is
only doing that.

00:08:02.891 --> 00:08:05.435
Okay. So, with
all of that a precursor,

00:08:05.436 --> 00:08:07.535
I want to talk about
a line of research.

00:08:07.536 --> 00:08:08.920
Today, I will talk
about a line of

00:08:08.921 --> 00:08:10.495
research I've been working
on with David Blei,

00:08:10.496 --> 00:08:13.820
who many of you know is a
computer scientist at Colombia,

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.535
whose goal is really to bring
together econometric models

00:08:18.536 --> 00:08:22.075
with Bayesian
probabilistic models

00:08:22.076 --> 00:08:23.820
to do counterfactual inference.

00:08:23.821 --> 00:08:25.970
Before I dive into that,
I'll mention that,

00:08:25.971 --> 00:08:28.410
and I have two strands

00:08:28.411 --> 00:08:30.570
of this machine learning and
causal inference literature,

00:08:30.571 --> 00:08:32.345
that most of my presentations and

00:08:32.346 --> 00:08:33.425
machine learning audiences have

00:08:33.426 --> 00:08:35.035
focus on the other strand,

00:08:35.036 --> 00:08:36.520
which is more about

00:08:36.521 --> 00:08:38.655
estimating heterogeneous
treatment effects,

00:08:38.656 --> 00:08:40.700
building things into
AB testing platform,

00:08:40.701 --> 00:08:42.880
personalized policy
evaluation, and recently,

00:08:42.881 --> 00:08:45.365
I've been thinking about
contextual bandits as well.

00:08:45.366 --> 00:08:47.285
So, that is one, I think,

00:08:47.286 --> 00:08:49.250
and a very easy place where

00:08:49.251 --> 00:08:52.560
machine learning and
causal inference come together.

00:08:52.561 --> 00:08:54.330
Because personalized
prediction and

00:08:54.331 --> 00:08:56.930
personalized treatment effects
are very close.

00:08:56.931 --> 00:08:58.365
Now, if you're not smart,

00:08:58.366 --> 00:09:01.170
you can get wildly wrong answers
when you make that leap.

00:09:01.171 --> 00:09:02.960
But if you're
a little bit clever and

00:09:02.961 --> 00:09:04.635
you incorporate
some basic insights,

00:09:04.636 --> 00:09:08.010
then you can modify
the techniques pretty,

00:09:08.011 --> 00:09:10.000
not too hard and

00:09:10.001 --> 00:09:12.815
get personalized treatment
effects and so on.

00:09:12.816 --> 00:09:14.375
So, that is one line,

00:09:14.376 --> 00:09:17.005
and I'm very excited about
that line and of course,

00:09:17.006 --> 00:09:19.520
MRA and others here are part of

00:09:19.521 --> 00:09:20.780
that broader literature as

00:09:20.781 --> 00:09:22.395
well as other folks in New York.

00:09:22.396 --> 00:09:25.170
This second line, is
fitting them with

00:09:25.171 --> 00:09:27.750
a different branch of economics,

00:09:27.751 --> 00:09:30.570
which we would call
a structural modeling,

00:09:30.571 --> 00:09:33.000
and this branch more naturally

00:09:33.001 --> 00:09:35.540
connects with people who
build probabilistic models.

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:37.360
Because we're writing down

00:09:37.361 --> 00:09:40.115
generative models of
how the world works,

00:09:40.116 --> 00:09:41.980
and I would say
the difference between

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:46.300
the economist and the
non-economists Bayesian,

00:09:46.301 --> 00:09:49.550
would be that the economists
are going to take a little

00:09:49.551 --> 00:09:50.995
bit more seriously the idea

00:09:50.996 --> 00:09:52.915
that we're estimating
preferences.

00:09:52.916 --> 00:09:55.090
We're estimating a
consumer's preferences.

00:09:55.091 --> 00:09:57.530
Therefore, we're really getting
something fundamental or

00:09:57.531 --> 00:10:00.415
structural about a person
or about a firm,

00:10:00.416 --> 00:10:02.690
so that we can take them
to new environments.

00:10:02.691 --> 00:10:05.785
But we have more complicated
applications of that.

00:10:05.786 --> 00:10:07.880
Auctions is one that I've
spent a lot of time on,

00:10:07.881 --> 00:10:10.200
where I assume that bidders
are maximizing profits,

00:10:10.201 --> 00:10:11.985
and they're playing equilibria.

00:10:11.986 --> 00:10:13.730
Those are more, I would

00:10:13.731 --> 00:10:16.310
a stronger assumption, stronger
behavioral assumptions.

00:10:16.311 --> 00:10:18.395
If you make those assumptions,
you can say things.

00:10:18.396 --> 00:10:19.695
The one I'm talking about today,

00:10:19.696 --> 00:10:20.985
all I'm going to be assuming,

00:10:20.986 --> 00:10:23.395
that consumers choose
what's best for them.

00:10:23.396 --> 00:10:26.380
So, it's a fairly weak
behavioral assumption.

00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:28.550
But nonetheless, those
assumptions can be

00:10:28.551 --> 00:10:31.255
used to do counterfactual
inferences.

00:10:31.256 --> 00:10:34.160
So, just that's how
this all fits in.

00:10:34.161 --> 00:10:35.270
So, I'm going to be fitting into

00:10:35.271 --> 00:10:37.500
a strand of the economics
literature that

00:10:37.501 --> 00:10:39.270
you guys might not
see as much unless

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:42.075
you've been listening to
Matt Tatty and Greg Louis.

00:10:42.076 --> 00:10:45.255
Okay. So, just to say that

00:10:45.256 --> 00:10:48.250
the class of datasets that
we're working with here,

00:10:48.251 --> 00:10:51.670
are we call in economics panel
or longitudinal datasets,

00:10:51.671 --> 00:10:55.520
where we're seeing a bunch
of units over time.

00:10:55.521 --> 00:10:58.050
So, example datasets of course,

00:10:58.051 --> 00:10:59.690
one of the things that
inspired this work,

00:10:59.691 --> 00:11:03.035
was work I was doing on
news using Microsoft data.

00:11:03.036 --> 00:11:05.800
You might have a set
of users and see

00:11:05.801 --> 00:11:07.250
the news articles they

00:11:07.251 --> 00:11:09.295
viewed or the websites
they viewed.

00:11:09.296 --> 00:11:11.380
One of the datasets
I'm going to look at

00:11:11.381 --> 00:11:13.490
today, is GPS data.

00:11:13.491 --> 00:11:16.780
So, I've got device ID for
millions of people and

00:11:16.781 --> 00:11:17.880
then I have a sequence of

00:11:17.881 --> 00:11:20.485
timestamp latitudes
and longitudes.

00:11:20.486 --> 00:11:23.300
That's the first dataset
I'll talk about today.

00:11:23.301 --> 00:11:26.375
Another dataset that I'm
starting to work with

00:11:26.376 --> 00:11:29.210
would be consumer
credit card transactions,

00:11:29.211 --> 00:11:31.670
where you see again
the consumer ID

00:11:31.671 --> 00:11:32.735
and the timestamp and the

00:11:32.736 --> 00:11:35.065
location and the amount
of the charge.

00:11:35.066 --> 00:11:37.845
Another dataset I'll look
at today, is shopping data.

00:11:37.846 --> 00:11:40.410
So, you see the consumer
ID and what they bought.

00:11:40.411 --> 00:11:43.870
So, the counterfactual
questions that we

00:11:43.871 --> 00:11:47.150
might think about are
in the new setting.

00:11:47.151 --> 00:11:48.800
We might say, well, how will

00:11:48.801 --> 00:11:50.950
users change their
consumption of news,

00:11:50.951 --> 00:11:53.605
if for example
Google News shuts down?

00:11:53.606 --> 00:11:55.855
Markus Mobius, who's
in MSR New England,

00:11:55.856 --> 00:11:57.420
and I wrote a paper about that,

00:11:57.421 --> 00:11:59.935
where Google News
shut down in Spain.

00:11:59.936 --> 00:12:02.615
So, we tried to make
predictions about,

00:12:02.616 --> 00:12:04.565
in this case, what
they would've done if

00:12:04.566 --> 00:12:06.745
Google News had not shut down.

00:12:06.746 --> 00:12:10.055
That's a counterfactual,
though we might actually have

00:12:10.056 --> 00:12:14.120
an actual event and then
we can verify our results.

00:12:14.121 --> 00:12:16.845
How do physical movements
of consumers change?

00:12:16.846 --> 00:12:18.355
If say, they say lose a job,

00:12:18.356 --> 00:12:19.760
or the one I'm going to focus on,

00:12:19.761 --> 00:12:21.730
is what happens if a
store opens and closes?

00:12:21.731 --> 00:12:22.910
How is that consumers
are going to

00:12:22.911 --> 00:12:24.570
change their shopping patterns?

00:12:24.571 --> 00:12:26.120
So, one thing I want to work on

00:12:26.121 --> 00:12:27.790
the future's like
the retail apocalypse.

00:12:27.791 --> 00:12:30.260
So, we see some of
these chain stores closing

00:12:30.261 --> 00:12:34.370
and what does that do to
consumer shopping behavior.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.670
Various other things,

00:12:36.671 --> 00:12:38.425
what products make
good loss-leaders,

00:12:38.426 --> 00:12:40.710
like what if the supermarkets

00:12:40.711 --> 00:12:42.620
going to to put
something on sale,

00:12:42.621 --> 00:12:43.950
what would be good
products to put on

00:12:43.951 --> 00:12:45.600
sale to attract people into

00:12:45.601 --> 00:12:46.790
the store and to attract

00:12:46.791 --> 00:12:48.745
particular types of
people into the store?

00:12:48.746 --> 00:12:50.580
So, the common features are that,

00:12:50.581 --> 00:12:52.940
we have some structured data
about the objects.

00:12:52.941 --> 00:12:54.730
In some cases, we'll have prices.

00:12:54.731 --> 00:12:55.620
But I'm generally going to be

00:12:55.621 --> 00:12:56.470
thinking about things where I

00:12:56.471 --> 00:12:58.690
don't have a lot of structured
data about the objects.

00:12:58.691 --> 00:13:02.120
The consumers consume maybe
a wide variety of products,

00:13:02.121 --> 00:13:04.750
and I want to understand
their choices.

00:13:04.751 --> 00:13:07.550
So, this is roughly

00:13:07.551 --> 00:13:10.010
related to the recommendation
system problem,

00:13:10.011 --> 00:13:12.035
which you guys have
all studied a lot.

00:13:12.036 --> 00:13:13.470
One approach to that,

00:13:13.471 --> 00:13:15.200
is a matrix
factorization approach.

00:13:15.201 --> 00:13:18.530
So, we can think about
a matrix of movies and users,

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:20.290
and we're going to
try to come up with,

00:13:20.291 --> 00:13:22.500
say of a factor structure,

00:13:22.501 --> 00:13:24.925
we can represent this matrix as

00:13:24.926 --> 00:13:28.695
a d-dimensional latent
user preferences

00:13:28.696 --> 00:13:30.585
for characteristics of movies.

00:13:30.586 --> 00:13:32.780
There's a d-dimensional
latent vector which

00:13:32.781 --> 00:13:34.950
describes the characteristics
of the movies,

00:13:34.951 --> 00:13:37.555
and I can predict the user's
preference for the movies

00:13:37.556 --> 00:13:41.160
by multiplying
those two matrices together.

00:13:41.161 --> 00:13:43.814
Broadly, this is
answering the question,

00:13:43.815 --> 00:13:45.975
what types of things
do users like?

00:13:45.976 --> 00:13:48.115
Of course, I know and
I'm very well aware

00:13:48.116 --> 00:13:49.150
that there are lots of extensions

00:13:49.151 --> 00:13:50.170
and generalizations of this,

00:13:50.171 --> 00:13:53.940
but just high level,this
simple matrix

00:13:53.941 --> 00:13:58.340
factorization can predict
what things they like.

00:13:58.341 --> 00:14:00.780
Of course, if you think about
from the perspective of

00:14:00.781 --> 00:14:04.010
Amazon or a company like that,

00:14:04.011 --> 00:14:06.400
doing this type of analysis
can be very simple.

00:14:06.401 --> 00:14:09.090
You can take the whole year's
worth of Amazon purchases

00:14:09.091 --> 00:14:12.925
and just boil it
down to a matrix.

00:14:12.926 --> 00:14:15.295
You're going to be losing
a lot of information though,

00:14:15.296 --> 00:14:18.120
when you boil that down
because you might be thrown

00:14:18.121 --> 00:14:19.305
away information about

00:14:19.306 --> 00:14:21.025
what did the consumer
buy together?

00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:24.770
Like maybe they bought hot dogs,

00:14:24.771 --> 00:14:27.170
and hot dog buns together
and I'll come back to that.

00:14:27.171 --> 00:14:30.565
That says something that's
more interesting that

00:14:30.566 --> 00:14:31.940
they bought them
together than if they

00:14:31.941 --> 00:14:34.120
bought them at different
times during the year.

00:14:34.121 --> 00:14:35.980
Maybe the product was

00:14:35.981 --> 00:14:37.530
on sale when they
bought that product.

00:14:37.531 --> 00:14:39.020
So, the reason they

00:14:39.021 --> 00:14:40.605
bought that product, is
because it was on sale.

00:14:40.606 --> 00:14:42.130
Maybe the reason
that they didn't buy

00:14:42.131 --> 00:14:44.255
a product was that's I
never showed it to them.

00:14:44.256 --> 00:14:46.680
My existing recommendation
system just didn't

00:14:46.681 --> 00:14:48.950
recommend this type of
product to this consumer.

00:14:48.951 --> 00:14:53.370
So, while this approach
is very helpful,

00:14:53.371 --> 00:14:56.690
if you start from here of
course it's completely

00:14:56.691 --> 00:14:57.940
impossible to answer questions

00:14:57.941 --> 00:14:59.550
about what would happen
if I change prices.

00:14:59.551 --> 00:15:00.965
Because you're not
really modeling

00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:03.320
why they bought
things to start with.

00:15:03.321 --> 00:15:05.620
The economics and marketing

00:15:05.621 --> 00:15:07.220
literatures and there's basically

00:15:07.221 --> 00:15:08.335
part of marketing that basically,

00:15:08.336 --> 00:15:10.510
is economics that we've
like computer science.

00:15:10.511 --> 00:15:11.530
We tend to invade and

00:15:11.531 --> 00:15:13.315
adjacent disciplines
and take them over.

00:15:13.316 --> 00:15:16.370
So, that's one of
the subjects of our invasion.

00:15:16.371 --> 00:15:18.620
So, we would typically build

00:15:18.621 --> 00:15:21.040
a functional form model
of the user utility,

00:15:21.041 --> 00:15:22.710
and estimate preferences based

00:15:22.711 --> 00:15:24.225
on the user choice behavior.

00:15:24.226 --> 00:15:27.285
But this literature which
like Daniel McFadden won

00:15:27.286 --> 00:15:31.770
a Nobel Prize for his work
on this in 1971 and 1972,

00:15:31.771 --> 00:15:33.800
so, this is
a very old literature,

00:15:33.801 --> 00:15:36.320
there he was modeling
the extension of the bark.

00:15:36.321 --> 00:15:39.120
So, he was modeling consumers
transportation choices.

00:15:39.121 --> 00:15:40.900
But same kind of setting,

00:15:40.901 --> 00:15:44.545
one product at the time like
different brands of yogurt,

00:15:44.546 --> 00:15:47.260
we typically have very simple
models like we have

00:15:47.261 --> 00:15:49.620
a single product
specific latent variable

00:15:49.621 --> 00:15:51.955
which we might think
of as product quality.

00:15:51.956 --> 00:15:54.010
Typically, in the old
days we didn't

00:15:54.011 --> 00:15:55.680
have micro-consumer data and

00:15:55.681 --> 00:15:57.340
so what we worried about was that

00:15:57.341 --> 00:15:59.180
that might be
correlated with price.

00:15:59.181 --> 00:16:00.785
So, we might worry that,

00:16:00.786 --> 00:16:03.355
if we just had
a single dataset with

00:16:03.356 --> 00:16:06.285
with constant prices
and the dataset, then,

00:16:06.286 --> 00:16:08.990
we don't know if
people are not buying

00:16:08.991 --> 00:16:10.170
this product because it has

00:16:10.171 --> 00:16:12.540
a high price or if it's
because it has low quality,

00:16:12.541 --> 00:16:13.850
and we might expect
generally the higher

00:16:13.851 --> 00:16:15.655
quality products
have higher prices.

00:16:15.656 --> 00:16:17.490
So, if we compare
a cross product,

00:16:17.491 --> 00:16:18.840
say here's a low price product

00:16:18.841 --> 00:16:20.310
and here's a high price product,

00:16:20.311 --> 00:16:22.250
that's not going to tell us
anything about what would

00:16:22.251 --> 00:16:24.660
happen if a single product
changed its price.

00:16:24.661 --> 00:16:26.640
So, people were
very worried about

00:16:26.641 --> 00:16:28.685
this latent variable of

00:16:28.686 --> 00:16:30.970
product quality that was
correlated with price.

00:16:30.971 --> 00:16:33.960
So, they spent a lot of time
trying to think about things

00:16:33.961 --> 00:16:37.100
that would shock prices
like cosh shocks and so on,

00:16:37.101 --> 00:16:38.565
that would move prices as

00:16:38.566 --> 00:16:40.120
a natural experiment and using

00:16:40.121 --> 00:16:42.455
that to try to learn about
the effects of price,

00:16:42.456 --> 00:16:45.570
but usually from aggregate data.

00:16:45.571 --> 00:16:47.390
Now, as we went along,

00:16:47.391 --> 00:16:48.840
we got more of this shock like

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:50.470
your scanner data
where we would see

00:16:50.471 --> 00:16:52.490
the same consumers over time

00:16:52.491 --> 00:16:53.850
and we would see the prices that

00:16:53.851 --> 00:16:58.335
individual consumers saw
when they visited the store.

00:16:58.336 --> 00:17:00.110
So, then it's
actually much easier

00:17:00.111 --> 00:17:01.890
to try to understand
the causal effect of

00:17:01.891 --> 00:17:03.600
price because I might

00:17:03.601 --> 00:17:05.670
see two essentially
identical consumers,

00:17:05.671 --> 00:17:07.255
one comes in on Tuesday,
one comes in on

00:17:07.256 --> 00:17:09.050
Wednesday the price goes

00:17:09.051 --> 00:17:10.955
down on Wednesday and

00:17:10.956 --> 00:17:13.100
they buy on Wednesday
and not on Tuesday.

00:17:13.101 --> 00:17:15.200
So, roughly it's not

00:17:15.201 --> 00:17:16.940
completely exogenous
variation but

00:17:16.941 --> 00:17:18.285
if you control for enough stuff,

00:17:18.286 --> 00:17:19.740
the idea is that you

00:17:19.741 --> 00:17:21.380
can think of whether you
came in on Tuesday or

00:17:21.381 --> 00:17:23.410
Wednesday before
the price change or after

00:17:23.411 --> 00:17:26.260
as good as random,

00:17:26.261 --> 00:17:29.100
and so that type of price
variation can then be used to

00:17:29.101 --> 00:17:30.680
understand the effect
of price in a way

00:17:30.681 --> 00:17:33.520
that's much harder to
do an aggregate data.

00:17:33.521 --> 00:17:36.265
Then, what this
literature did is it

00:17:36.266 --> 00:17:39.130
actually stack with
this one product

00:17:39.131 --> 00:17:40.235
at a time thing but it

00:17:40.236 --> 00:17:41.630
actually went off
in a whole bunch

00:17:41.631 --> 00:17:42.845
of other directions which

00:17:42.846 --> 00:17:44.190
actually relate now to

00:17:44.191 --> 00:17:46.475
the inverse reinforcement
learning literature.

00:17:46.476 --> 00:17:47.990
They would basically assume,

00:17:47.991 --> 00:17:49.800
suppose this consumer is solving

00:17:49.801 --> 00:17:51.210
a dynamic programming problem

00:17:51.211 --> 00:17:52.970
and they're stockpiling
laundry detergent.

00:17:52.971 --> 00:17:55.390
So one of my PhD students wrote

00:17:55.391 --> 00:17:58.220
their whole thesis on well,

00:17:58.221 --> 00:18:00.895
one was stockpiling laundry
detergent another was like

00:18:00.896 --> 00:18:02.820
experimentation versus habit

00:18:02.821 --> 00:18:04.355
formation in laundry detergent.

00:18:04.356 --> 00:18:06.200
So, we're modeling
all these consumers

00:18:06.201 --> 00:18:08.005
solving dynamic
programming problems while

00:18:08.006 --> 00:18:09.950
estimating the value function and

00:18:09.951 --> 00:18:12.405
inferring their dynamic
preferences from that.

00:18:12.406 --> 00:18:14.245
So, that literature we went to a

00:18:14.246 --> 00:18:16.310
very I would say
structural direction,

00:18:16.311 --> 00:18:19.375
this was sort of controversial
because people didn't like

00:18:19.376 --> 00:18:21.245
all the assumptions
and like why is it

00:18:21.246 --> 00:18:23.640
habit formation here
and stockpiling there?

00:18:23.641 --> 00:18:26.935
Why are you stockpiling in
this way and not in that way?

00:18:26.936 --> 00:18:29.120
So, it was controversial

00:18:29.121 --> 00:18:30.515
because of all the assumptions
but that's sort of

00:18:30.516 --> 00:18:31.860
the direction the literature went

00:18:31.861 --> 00:18:34.915
in which is a very
different direction

00:18:34.916 --> 00:18:36.090
than we think about

00:18:36.091 --> 00:18:37.870
these big recommendation
system models

00:18:37.871 --> 00:18:39.950
where you're looking
at lots of products.

00:18:39.951 --> 00:18:41.765
One product at time misses

00:18:41.766 --> 00:18:43.435
many aspects of consumer choice,

00:18:43.436 --> 00:18:46.730
for example, actually if you
ask like safe, I'm sorry.

00:18:46.731 --> 00:18:48.485
An unnamed grocery store
gave you this data

00:18:48.486 --> 00:18:50.725
actually back in the early 2000's

00:18:50.726 --> 00:18:52.960
and the grocery store

00:18:52.961 --> 00:18:55.285
actually the problem they
came to was they said,

00:18:55.286 --> 00:18:57.115
"I want to understand how
to segment my consumers,

00:18:57.116 --> 00:18:59.910
and I want to understand
how to give them coupons,

00:18:59.911 --> 00:19:02.180
and how to do these price
promotions and discounts."

00:19:02.181 --> 00:19:03.050
But they were thinking

00:19:03.051 --> 00:19:04.978
about like store-to-store
competition.

00:19:04.979 --> 00:19:06.800
They were thinking that I want
to get the consumers into

00:19:06.801 --> 00:19:09.020
the store and see and they
would buy other things.

00:19:09.021 --> 00:19:10.925
So, those types of questions

00:19:10.926 --> 00:19:13.905
are really not answered
by just studying yoghurt.

00:19:13.906 --> 00:19:15.605
In your studying yoghurt,

00:19:15.606 --> 00:19:16.985
you can understand the effect of

00:19:16.986 --> 00:19:18.615
coupons or something or TV ads

00:19:18.616 --> 00:19:19.860
but it's hard to understand

00:19:19.861 --> 00:19:22.565
the things that are
interesting to a store.

00:19:22.566 --> 00:19:24.735
Organizing the store and so on.

00:19:24.736 --> 00:19:26.935
Then in the online space,

00:19:26.936 --> 00:19:28.770
there's sort of the online
version of shelf space.

00:19:28.771 --> 00:19:30.215
So, what should I show consumers?

00:19:30.216 --> 00:19:32.800
What should I recommend
to them on the page?

00:19:32.801 --> 00:19:34.565
What kinds of discounts
would get what,

00:19:34.566 --> 00:19:36.035
if I email them a discount offer

00:19:36.036 --> 00:19:37.760
what's going to get them
to come to my website,

00:19:37.761 --> 00:19:38.990
but if they come to my website

00:19:38.991 --> 00:19:40.290
they're going to
buy other things.

00:19:40.291 --> 00:19:43.600
So there are some of my
colleagues did some work at eBay,

00:19:43.601 --> 00:19:48.260
where they sort of
found that when

00:19:48.261 --> 00:19:50.580
eBay sort of made
changes that made it

00:19:50.581 --> 00:19:53.105
cheaper for sellers
to listings on eBay,

00:19:53.106 --> 00:19:54.960
in fact those sellers

00:19:54.961 --> 00:19:56.580
bought stuff when
they came to eBay,

00:19:56.581 --> 00:19:59.045
and so just getting the sellers
to come to eBay increased

00:19:59.046 --> 00:20:00.190
their revenue because the sellers

00:20:00.191 --> 00:20:01.765
bought stuff while they
were on the store.

00:20:01.766 --> 00:20:03.770
So, the spillover effect
is very big,

00:20:03.771 --> 00:20:06.345
and Marks and Matrice for
member back in the eBay used

00:20:06.346 --> 00:20:08.945
to put a lot of spam ads on Bing

00:20:08.946 --> 00:20:09.970
and part of that is
because if you could

00:20:09.971 --> 00:20:11.630
just get that
consumer to click and

00:20:11.631 --> 00:20:12.740
come to eBay even if

00:20:12.741 --> 00:20:14.405
the ad had nothing to
do with the query,

00:20:14.406 --> 00:20:15.735
maybe they'd buy something.

00:20:15.736 --> 00:20:17.400
So, this idea of
getting people into

00:20:17.401 --> 00:20:19.190
the store matters for that.

00:20:19.191 --> 00:20:20.710
So, what we're going to do in

00:20:20.711 --> 00:20:23.150
this line of research is we're
going to try to bring in

00:20:23.151 --> 00:20:26.230
matrix factorization and
embedding Tet methods from

00:20:26.231 --> 00:20:30.165
CS and we're going to
use a Bayesian approach,

00:20:30.166 --> 00:20:32.160
it doesn't have to
be Bayesian but

00:20:32.161 --> 00:20:34.225
we liked it for a
variety of reasons.

00:20:34.226 --> 00:20:36.610
We get flexibility in
incorporating the structure.

00:20:36.611 --> 00:20:39.120
We can also represent
the uncertainty that for

00:20:39.121 --> 00:20:40.865
some consumers we
don't really know very

00:20:40.866 --> 00:20:42.845
much and for other
consumers we know more,

00:20:42.846 --> 00:20:44.300
and we can ensure using

00:20:44.301 --> 00:20:46.150
priors that the consumers
if we don't know

00:20:46.151 --> 00:20:49.835
very much we can learn
using hierarchical priors.

00:20:49.836 --> 00:20:52.195
So, we're going to pay,

00:20:52.196 --> 00:20:53.440
we're going to estimate an

00:20:53.441 --> 00:20:55.315
economic style structural model

00:20:55.316 --> 00:20:58.155
using computational techniques
from Machine Learning,

00:20:58.156 --> 00:20:59.470
and we're going to be paying

00:20:59.471 --> 00:21:01.585
particular attention
to identification.

00:21:01.586 --> 00:21:04.700
So, identification is
the economics jargon for,

00:21:04.701 --> 00:21:06.825
can you really learn
something from this?

00:21:06.826 --> 00:21:08.065
Like is there
actually variation in

00:21:08.066 --> 00:21:09.895
prices and helps you
learn that and also,

00:21:09.896 --> 00:21:12.860
we're going tune our models
along counterfactuals.

00:21:12.861 --> 00:21:14.910
So, we're going to try to
select models that do well on

00:21:14.911 --> 00:21:16.790
counterfactuals
rather than models

00:21:16.791 --> 00:21:18.235
that do well in
freight prediction.

00:21:18.236 --> 00:21:19.590
So, I'll try to flesh that out.

00:21:19.591 --> 00:21:20.750
I would say generally though,

00:21:20.751 --> 00:21:23.600
that is a theme that
I think can be taken

00:21:23.601 --> 00:21:26.715
away to the machine
learning people,

00:21:26.716 --> 00:21:30.265
maybe in socially, the
canonical Machine Learning,

00:21:30.266 --> 00:21:31.450
we often just think okay,

00:21:31.451 --> 00:21:32.280
we want to predict well,

00:21:32.281 --> 00:21:33.480
we're going to look
at goodness of fit.

00:21:33.481 --> 00:21:35.195
This mean squared
error log likelihood,

00:21:35.196 --> 00:21:37.980
but if what you're actually
going to do is think about

00:21:37.981 --> 00:21:39.510
changing a price than instead

00:21:39.511 --> 00:21:41.115
of tuning the model
for goodness of fit.

00:21:41.116 --> 00:21:43.790
I should hold out some
price changes and see

00:21:43.791 --> 00:21:46.430
how well I do with these kind
of counterfactual exercises,

00:21:46.431 --> 00:21:49.375
and so those types of getting
the right optimization,

00:21:49.376 --> 00:21:50.535
the right objective function

00:21:50.536 --> 00:21:51.870
can really make a difference.

00:21:51.871 --> 00:21:53.355
So, there's two ways
you can use this.

00:21:53.356 --> 00:21:55.450
We can use it the way we
do in economics normally,

00:21:55.451 --> 00:21:57.615
but if I think about
at a large scale,

00:21:57.616 --> 00:21:59.860
it can also be
a pre-processing step.

00:21:59.861 --> 00:22:02.285
So, we might be able to take,

00:22:02.286 --> 00:22:04.440
they're going to be
some scaling issues with this.

00:22:04.441 --> 00:22:06.690
So, I might be able
to learn some things

00:22:06.691 --> 00:22:08.120
