00:23:21.771 --> 00:23:23.930
how many skew should
there be in surface?

00:23:23.931 --> 00:23:27.595
Or how many versions of
sequel should we offer?

00:23:27.596 --> 00:23:29.585
Questions like that
they would sort of fit

00:23:29.586 --> 00:23:32.605
into this type of
a framework as well.

00:23:32.606 --> 00:23:35.630
So, we're going to start
because we have got

00:23:35.631 --> 00:23:37.150
computer science
colleagues have gotten

00:23:37.151 --> 00:23:40.140
nice little acronyms
for all of my model.

00:23:40.141 --> 00:23:41.895
So, we call this the Travel Time

00:23:41.896 --> 00:23:44.445
Factorization Model
of user choice.

00:23:44.446 --> 00:23:46.400
But in the economics framework,

00:23:46.401 --> 00:23:48.310
we start by writing
down a utility.

00:23:48.311 --> 00:23:50.100
That's just the
convention we have.

00:23:50.101 --> 00:23:52.085
So, it's going to map

00:23:52.086 --> 00:23:54.640
into a probabilistic
model like one for one,

00:23:54.641 --> 00:23:57.020
but we usually just start
by writing down utility.

00:23:57.021 --> 00:24:00.840
So, we say the user's utility
has these components.

00:24:00.841 --> 00:24:02.590
There's a popularity component

00:24:02.591 --> 00:24:04.650
for item i, for restaurant i.

00:24:04.651 --> 00:24:06.755
Then there's the
consumer preferences

00:24:06.756 --> 00:24:09.010
which are a product
of latent factors.

00:24:09.011 --> 00:24:12.810
So, this user u has a vector
of latent preferences and

00:24:12.811 --> 00:24:14.030
a restaurant i is going to

00:24:14.031 --> 00:24:17.440
have a vector of latent factors.

00:24:17.441 --> 00:24:19.300
Then this is the part

00:24:19.301 --> 00:24:20.985
that gets a little
more I would say,

00:24:20.986 --> 00:24:22.520
structural although it's I

00:24:22.521 --> 00:24:24.005
mean I don't want to
glorify it too much,

00:24:24.006 --> 00:24:25.490
it's just that we're doing

00:24:25.491 --> 00:24:27.235
sort of tuning on the model here.

00:24:27.236 --> 00:24:29.930
We're going to say that
you're going to care about

00:24:29.931 --> 00:24:33.210
the distance between
user u item i.

00:24:33.211 --> 00:24:37.025
So, this covariate varies
at the user item level,

00:24:37.026 --> 00:24:39.535
and we're gong to put
it in a log format

00:24:39.536 --> 00:24:40.200
because there's sort of

00:24:40.201 --> 00:24:41.985
some concavity we
think with distance,

00:24:41.986 --> 00:24:44.325
and then we're going to
factorize the coefficients.

00:24:44.326 --> 00:24:47.090
So, we're going to say
that there's a user

00:24:47.091 --> 00:24:50.125
items specific preference
for distance,

00:24:50.126 --> 00:24:52.300
but that's going to be
represented in latent factors.

00:24:52.301 --> 00:24:53.925
So, what that's going to
allow us to say is that;

00:24:53.926 --> 00:24:56.535
I'm not willing to travel
very far for coffee,

00:24:56.536 --> 00:24:59.650
but I am willing to
travel for good food.

00:24:59.651 --> 00:25:01.120
So, if you think about it

00:25:01.121 --> 00:25:02.575
like, so we're going
to find later,

00:25:02.576 --> 00:25:04.775
is that people are willing
to travel farther in

00:25:04.776 --> 00:25:06.910
the Bay area for like
Asian restaurants

00:25:06.911 --> 00:25:08.620
but they don't travel for coffee.

00:25:08.621 --> 00:25:10.690
But of course there's
lots of restaurants.

00:25:10.691 --> 00:25:12.910
So, I I can't possibly
learn my user

00:25:12.911 --> 00:25:15.400
items specific preference
at that level.

00:25:15.401 --> 00:25:17.020
So again, my factorizing it,

00:25:17.021 --> 00:25:19.260
I can figure out there are
types of restaurants that

00:25:19.261 --> 00:25:22.170
users are willing to drive to
and types that they're not.

00:25:22.171 --> 00:25:24.295
Then there's a time
varying effect which could

00:25:24.296 --> 00:25:25.330
capture that maybe some

00:25:25.331 --> 00:25:26.700
restaurants are
good when it rains,

00:25:26.701 --> 00:25:28.005
and some restaurants
are good when it

00:25:28.006 --> 00:25:29.315
doesn't rain or
different times of

00:25:29.316 --> 00:25:33.150
year with students, and
then we have noise.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:37.700
Then, we have
a hierarchical Bayesian

00:25:37.701 --> 00:25:38.735
models they're going to affect

00:25:38.736 --> 00:25:41.380
the mean of the prior of
these different things.

00:25:41.381 --> 00:25:44.465
So, we have say a 160
restaurant types,

00:25:44.466 --> 00:25:46.575
we can't learn
each user's preference for

00:25:46.576 --> 00:25:48.175
160 things because the users

00:25:48.176 --> 00:25:49.810
only go to like 12 restaurants,

00:25:49.811 --> 00:25:52.010
but I can learn that

00:25:52.011 --> 00:25:53.390
the latent vector for

00:25:53.391 --> 00:25:54.900
Japanese restaurants is similar

00:25:54.901 --> 00:25:56.260
to that for Chinese restaurants,

00:25:56.261 --> 00:25:57.430
and that's going to go into the

00:25:57.431 --> 00:26:00.315
hierarchical prior on the Alphas.

00:26:00.316 --> 00:26:03.680
So, this is the way we would
write it in economics,

00:26:03.681 --> 00:26:05.160
and it turns out that it's fairly

00:26:05.161 --> 00:26:06.680
straightforward to show that

00:26:06.681 --> 00:26:09.765
if the consumer is just
choosing the maximum of those,

00:26:09.766 --> 00:26:11.000
if you assume that the consumer

00:26:11.001 --> 00:26:12.355
choose the maximum of those,

00:26:12.356 --> 00:26:16.140
then the probability
that user U and time T

00:26:16.141 --> 00:26:20.550
chooses item I is
this multinomial logit format.

00:26:20.551 --> 00:26:22.520
So, yeah.

00:26:22.521 --> 00:26:24.260
&gt;&gt; For that to continue with

00:26:24.261 --> 00:26:26.335
an assumption on
the noises distribution?

00:26:26.336 --> 00:26:28.910
&gt;&gt; Yes. Exactly so the error
needs to be a logit error,

00:26:28.911 --> 00:26:30.040
it needs to be extreme value,

00:26:30.041 --> 00:26:31.515
type one extreme value error.

00:26:31.516 --> 00:26:31.840
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:26:31.841 --> 00:26:33.855
&gt;&gt; So, if it's a type
one extreme value error

00:26:33.856 --> 00:26:36.460
then we get this functional form.

00:26:36.461 --> 00:26:39.260
So, of course in

00:26:39.261 --> 00:26:41.280
machine learning you would
have just said it starting,

00:26:41.281 --> 00:26:43.030
and so I'm going to write down

00:26:43.031 --> 00:26:45.695
a multinomial logit
probabilistic model.

00:26:45.696 --> 00:26:48.105
In economics we just say well,

00:26:48.106 --> 00:26:51.120
it's convenient to know
that if a consumer is

00:26:51.121 --> 00:26:54.565
maximizing utility and
their utility looks like this,

00:26:54.566 --> 00:26:56.690
then the parameters
you learned from doing

00:26:56.691 --> 00:26:58.110
this estimation are actually

00:26:58.111 --> 00:26:59.700
the perimeters of
their utility function.

00:26:59.701 --> 00:27:00.990
Then you say well,

00:27:00.991 --> 00:27:02.145
why do I care?

00:27:02.146 --> 00:27:03.450
Well, if you're going
to predict in the same

00:27:03.451 --> 00:27:05.120
environment you don't care.

00:27:05.121 --> 00:27:08.030
Right? If the environment
is stable then you don't

00:27:08.031 --> 00:27:10.630
need to care why the
consumer did what they did,

00:27:10.631 --> 00:27:12.285
all you need to know
is what they did.

00:27:12.286 --> 00:27:15.950
But if I change the
distance or if I make

00:27:15.951 --> 00:27:21.470
a particular restaurant
unavailable or if something,

00:27:21.471 --> 00:27:22.910
if I put a different
restaurant in

00:27:22.911 --> 00:27:24.965
