down through the Silicon
Valley to San Jose.

00:32:21.371 --> 00:32:22.390
We're going to call

00:32:22.391 --> 00:32:24.150
a lunch restaurant visit
is if we observed

00:32:24.151 --> 00:32:26.240
at least two pings more than
three minutes apart

00:32:26.241 --> 00:32:28.370
between 11:30AM and 1:30 PM.

00:32:28.371 --> 00:32:30.195
This also really kills
us because a lot of,

00:32:30.196 --> 00:32:32.300
we don't have a ton of
pings and so a lot of

00:32:32.301 --> 00:32:33.390
people ping once but

00:32:33.391 --> 00:32:34.855
they could have just
been walking by.

00:32:34.856 --> 00:32:37.750
So, with more work we could
try to do more to get

00:32:37.751 --> 00:32:41.225
more pings back in but
we just stuck with this.

00:32:41.226 --> 00:32:43.520
Then the restaurants were
identified using data that

00:32:43.521 --> 00:32:45.660
Yelp provided us that
uses geo-coordinates,

00:32:45.661 --> 00:32:47.130
star ratings, price range,

00:32:47.131 --> 00:32:49.640
as well as the restaurant
opening and closing times.

00:32:49.641 --> 00:32:52.820
So, in our data we have,

00:32:52.821 --> 00:32:56.870
per user we have about 12
visits per user and

00:32:56.871 --> 00:32:59.080
again this is the selected sample

00:32:59.081 --> 00:33:01.245
of users that had
plenty of visits.

00:33:01.246 --> 00:33:03.780
They visited seven
distinct restaurants,

00:33:03.781 --> 00:33:05.200
so just because I
see the user I don't

00:33:05.201 --> 00:33:06.980
exactly know where
they're going to go next.

00:33:06.981 --> 00:33:11.520
Each restaurant is in
multiple Yelp categories,

00:33:11.521 --> 00:33:12.530
so they actually visited

00:33:12.531 --> 00:33:14.715
more than seven
distinct categories.

00:33:14.716 --> 00:33:17.350
They went on a median distance

00:33:17.351 --> 00:33:18.670
of three miles to
the restaurants,

00:33:18.671 --> 00:33:19.440
so actually traveling a little

00:33:19.441 --> 00:33:20.380
farther than I thought they

00:33:20.381 --> 00:33:22.930
would, and so on.

00:33:22.931 --> 00:33:24.450
From the restaurant perspective,

00:33:24.451 --> 00:33:28.120
the restaurants, the
data's kind of sparse.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:32.180
Once we narrow down
to a small user base,

00:33:32.181 --> 00:33:33.360
then we're not going to have

00:33:33.361 --> 00:33:34.975
a lot of visits per restaurant.

00:33:34.976 --> 00:33:37.730
So, our restaurants only
get about 14 visits per

00:33:37.731 --> 00:33:40.890
restaurant and so on.

00:33:40.891 --> 00:33:43.290
Okay. So, then the estimation,

00:33:43.291 --> 00:33:45.910
we do Bayesian estimation
with a hierarchical prior.

00:33:45.911 --> 00:33:47.250
There's a Gaussian prior over

00:33:47.251 --> 00:33:49.695
the latent characteristics
which are shifted

00:33:49.696 --> 00:33:52.390
by the observed covariates

00:33:52.391 --> 00:33:55.090
like the ratings and
the price categories,

00:33:55.091 --> 00:33:57.450
and the restaurant types.

00:33:57.451 --> 00:34:00.149
Then we estimate the coefficients

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:02.630
on those characteristics,

00:34:02.631 --> 00:34:05.230
these matrices H. We use

00:34:05.231 --> 00:34:06.850
mean-field variational inference

00:34:06.851 --> 00:34:08.420
to approximate the posterior.

00:34:08.421 --> 00:34:10.690
We're using independent
Gaussians and we

00:34:10.691 --> 00:34:13.900
find the parameters that
minimize the distance.

00:34:13.901 --> 00:34:16.280
It's yet to be seen how

00:34:16.281 --> 00:34:18.280
much people are going to
hate this in economics,

00:34:18.281 --> 00:34:20.100
because we have typically used

00:34:20.101 --> 00:34:23.340
Markov chain Monte Carlo
in economics,

00:34:23.341 --> 00:34:26.430
and people have felt
strongly about this type

00:34:26.431 --> 00:34:29.535
of approximation has
not been sort of,

00:34:29.536 --> 00:34:31.855
I would say accepted yet.

00:34:31.856 --> 00:34:35.340
I'm testing the waters by
putting it out there and so,

00:34:35.341 --> 00:34:37.590
we will see how much
people complain about it,

00:34:37.591 --> 00:34:41.040
but I think my response
to that is, historically,

00:34:41.041 --> 00:34:42.500
economists thought about as,

00:34:42.501 --> 00:34:43.950
we were going to
write down a model,

00:34:43.951 --> 00:34:45.220
we were going to say the God of

00:34:45.221 --> 00:34:46.735
economics gave us the model.

00:34:46.736 --> 00:34:48.530
We were going to get
all the functional forms,

00:34:48.531 --> 00:34:50.720
and we were going to get it
sort of perfectly right,

00:34:50.721 --> 00:34:52.385
and then we're
going to do sort of

00:34:52.386 --> 00:34:54.435
theories is going to tell us,

00:34:54.436 --> 00:34:56.810
and statistical theory
will tell us if it's right

00:34:56.811 --> 00:34:59.535
and will get confidence
intervals and standard errors.

00:34:59.536 --> 00:35:01.800
While the machine learning
perspective is a little more,

00:35:01.801 --> 00:35:04.250
we're going to hold out
test data and see if it works.

00:35:04.251 --> 00:35:08.270
Now, that's not as helpful
for counter-factual imprints.

00:35:08.271 --> 00:35:09.530
But so, what we're
going to try to

00:35:09.531 --> 00:35:11.420
do is find datasets that have

00:35:11.421 --> 00:35:12.870
lots and lots of experiments in

00:35:12.871 --> 00:35:14.765
them or natural experiments,

00:35:14.766 --> 00:35:16.830
so that we can hold out test data

00:35:16.831 --> 00:35:19.080
of experiments and see
if it works and then

00:35:19.081 --> 00:35:21.860
that will hopefully
convince the people who

00:35:21.861 --> 00:35:23.290
were used to having theorems that

00:35:23.291 --> 00:35:25.200
say that they're getting
the right answer.

00:35:25.201 --> 00:35:27.030
Because if you're approximating

00:35:27.031 --> 00:35:28.825
the posterior with
independent Gaussians,

00:35:28.826 --> 00:35:30.615
obviously you're not
getting the right answer.

00:35:30.616 --> 00:35:33.430
So, I can't prove
a theorem that says that I

00:35:33.431 --> 00:35:38.215
am but it works well enough.

00:35:38.216 --> 00:35:41.810
Okay. So, in terms of
Model Fit, so again,

00:35:41.811 --> 00:35:43.550
you guys probably are
less interested in

00:35:43.551 --> 00:35:46.675
this really kind of stupid
multinomial benchmark.

00:35:46.676 --> 00:35:48.180
Stupid in the sense
it doesn't have

00:35:48.181 --> 00:35:49.780
a lot of parameters
and heterogeneity,

00:35:49.781 --> 00:35:51.480
this is again, more
of a benchmark

00:35:51.481 --> 00:35:53.535
up against the classic
economics approach.

00:35:53.536 --> 00:35:57.430
So, I'll just show you our
model in the test sample.

00:35:57.431 --> 00:36:01.010
We get our precision at
one is about 20 percent,

00:36:01.011 --> 00:36:04.360
our precision at
five is 35 percent,

00:36:04.361 --> 00:36:05.740
and at 10 is 42 percent.

00:36:05.741 --> 00:36:08.455
So, we're doing
a reasonably good job

00:36:08.456 --> 00:36:12.780
of predicting what
users are choosing.

00:36:12.781 --> 00:36:16.300
We break out our goodness of
fit in a variety of ways,

00:36:16.301 --> 00:36:19.880
to show basically how this
works with user deciles.

00:36:19.881 --> 00:36:22.100
So, here's an example
in the test set,

00:36:22.101 --> 00:36:24.980
the precision at one is
going to be much higher for

00:36:24.981 --> 00:36:29.395
the 10th decile of
user restaurant consumption.

00:36:29.396 --> 00:36:31.440
The users who use a lot were much

00:36:31.441 --> 00:36:33.900
better and so the model
is able to adapt to

00:36:33.901 --> 00:36:36.150
the more frequent users and
learn their preferences

00:36:36.151 --> 00:36:38.590
better and same with restaurants.

00:36:38.591 --> 00:36:39.860
Restaurants are more popular,

00:36:39.861 --> 00:36:43.820
we're able to also predict
those better and so on.

00:36:43.821 --> 00:36:46.850
This picture we're looking
at how we do in terms of

00:36:46.851 --> 00:36:49.275
fitting the how distance

00:36:49.276 --> 00:36:51.155
relates to the visit probability.

00:36:51.156 --> 00:36:54.650
So, the actual relationship
is here in red,

00:36:54.651 --> 00:36:56.820
so this is the
visit probabilities

00:36:56.821 --> 00:36:58.180
of a function of
the user's distance

00:36:58.181 --> 00:36:59.740
for the restaurant and we

00:36:59.741 --> 00:37:01.690
see that actually in
this aggregate way,

00:37:01.691 --> 00:37:04.670
both the simple
multinomial logit and

00:37:04.671 --> 00:37:07.360
the personalized TTFM
multinomial logit

00:37:07.361 --> 00:37:09.205
both fit the shape pretty well.

00:37:09.206 --> 00:37:11.420
So, actually one insight is that,

00:37:11.421 --> 00:37:13.040
actually we are talking
about this earlier

00:37:13.041 --> 00:37:15.370
with some colleagues
here is that,

00:37:15.371 --> 00:37:18.220
whether you want a complicated
machine learning model

00:37:18.221 --> 00:37:19.750
or a complicated
personalize model

00:37:19.751 --> 00:37:21.030
depends on the question
you're asking,

00:37:21.031 --> 00:37:23.220
and if you're just want to
ask a simple question like

00:37:23.221 --> 00:37:25.310
how does distance from users

00:37:25.311 --> 00:37:27.755
on average relate to probability,

00:37:27.756 --> 00:37:31.520
then actually a simple model
can do that on average

00:37:31.521 --> 00:37:35.830
and it's a little less bumpy
than my complicated model.

00:37:35.831 --> 00:37:39.210
But if you want to ask how
will this user respond,

00:37:39.211 --> 00:37:41.060
then of course you need
a more personalized models.

00:37:41.061 --> 00:37:42.900
So, in some sense
if we were tuning

00:37:42.901 --> 00:37:45.535
for just this kind of
aggregate prediction,

00:37:45.536 --> 00:37:47.840
we should throw away all the
latent variables and choose

00:37:47.841 --> 00:37:49.200
a very simple model but if

00:37:49.201 --> 00:37:50.905
I'm tuning for personalization,

00:37:50.906 --> 00:37:52.865
then I should use
a more complex model.

00:37:52.866 --> 00:37:54.500
I pause on that,

00:37:54.501 --> 00:37:56.100
because actually a lot
of economic questions

00:37:56.101 --> 00:37:57.765
actually are a little bit
more aggregate.

00:37:57.766 --> 00:38:00.310
I might be raising a price
for a segment of consumers,

00:38:00.311 --> 00:38:02.360
or making an offer for
a group of consumers,

00:38:02.361 --> 00:38:06.320
and then you don't necessarily
want full personalization.

00:38:06.321 --> 00:38:11.360
Okay. Then let me now show you
just a few of the results.

00:38:11.361 --> 00:38:14.800
So, first of all, we
compute elasticity,

00:38:14.801 --> 00:38:15.960
so elasticity is how

00:38:15.961 --> 00:38:17.400
a percentage change in X

00:38:17.401 --> 00:38:19.320
leads to a percentage
change in Y,

00:38:19.321 --> 00:38:20.420
so now we're looking
at how does a

00:38:20.421 --> 00:38:22.010
percentage change
in distance lead

00:38:22.011 --> 00:38:24.150
to a percentage change
in visit probability.

00:38:24.151 --> 00:38:26.210
So, these elasticities,

00:38:26.211 --> 00:38:27.310
they're negative because there's

00:38:27.311 --> 00:38:28.295
some things farther away,

00:38:28.296 --> 00:38:30.250
you're less likely
to visit and we can

00:38:30.251 --> 00:38:33.235
see here just ranking Mexican,

00:38:33.236 --> 00:38:35.940
you're very sensitive
to distance, while say,

00:38:35.941 --> 00:38:39.680
Chinese and Japanese you're
less sensitive to distance.

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:42.890
Your more sensitive, we
don't actually have a lot

00:38:42.891 --> 00:38:44.580
of price range threes
and fours at lunch,

00:38:44.581 --> 00:38:47.080
so mostly the one versus
two is where we have data.

00:38:47.081 --> 00:38:48.900
So, you're less
willing to travel for

00:38:48.901 --> 00:38:50.675
cheaper restaurants and you're

00:38:50.676 --> 00:38:54.445
less willing to travel for
low rated restaurants. Yeah.

00:38:54.446 --> 00:38:56.370
&gt;&gt; Will that be
explained by variability

00:38:56.371 --> 00:38:58.725
within each category?

00:38:58.726 --> 00:39:04.115
&gt;&gt; So, how would you
actually distinguish this?

00:39:04.116 --> 00:39:06.190
Yeah, so basically, the way that

00:39:06.191 --> 00:39:08.385
we would tease these
apart would be,

00:39:08.386 --> 00:39:10.000
there's some Chinese
restaurants that

00:39:10.001 --> 00:39:11.605
are in one versus two.

00:39:11.606 --> 00:39:13.140
But as I mentioned earlier,

00:39:13.141 --> 00:39:15.615
we don't have experimental
variation in this stuff,

00:39:15.616 --> 00:39:18.125
so I wouldn't interpret
these completely.

00:39:18.126 --> 00:39:20.250
I wouldn't go out and make
business decisions based on

00:39:20.251 --> 00:39:21.310
these results but I would say

00:39:21.311 --> 00:39:23.590
that there's sort of descriptive.

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:28.000
These are getting estimated as

00:39:28.001 --> 00:39:29.690
turns of the loadings
on these variables

00:39:29.691 --> 00:39:32.825
and the hierarchical prior. Yeah.

00:39:32.826 --> 00:39:34.890
&gt;&gt; So, the previous plots
I interpreted

00:39:34.891 --> 00:39:36.750
those as just you set up

00:39:36.751 --> 00:39:38.600
this ranking problem
where your way to rank

00:39:38.601 --> 00:39:40.485
whether utilities
predictive by a model.

00:39:40.486 --> 00:39:40.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:39:40.821 --> 00:39:42.855
&gt;&gt; Then check the position
at one position.

00:39:42.856 --> 00:39:43.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:39:43.671 --> 00:39:44.900
&gt;&gt; But I wasn't sure that

00:39:44.901 --> 00:39:47.440
the model as stated was
actually identified like that,

00:39:47.441 --> 00:39:49.500
you couldn't pin down
the parameters of

00:39:49.501 --> 00:39:51.630
that model to within

00:39:51.631 --> 00:39:53.490
Epsilon of the ground
truth parameters if

00:39:53.491 --> 00:39:56.480
the users were actually behaving
according to that model.

00:39:56.481 --> 00:39:58.200
So, I don't know what to
make of these numbers,

00:39:58.201 --> 00:39:59.455
like it could well be that,

00:39:59.456 --> 00:40:00.190
I could come up with

00:40:00.191 --> 00:40:02.480
two alternative
explanations that just

00:40:02.481 --> 00:40:05.720
my optimizations happen to
pick one of those two date.

00:40:05.721 --> 00:40:07.210
&gt;&gt; Yes, so I would say here

00:40:07.211 --> 00:40:10.570
the identification is
less clean and as I

00:40:10.571 --> 00:40:13.560
said you could find
another way to fit

00:40:13.561 --> 00:40:15.000
that would load up
more on some of

00:40:15.001 --> 00:40:16.815
these factors and not on others.

00:40:16.816 --> 00:40:19.010
I'm going to show you in
a minute the way that we try to

00:40:19.011 --> 00:40:21.765
test it with restaurants
opening and closing,

00:40:21.766 --> 00:40:23.160
but this dataset was too small,

00:40:23.161 --> 00:40:24.640
so we didn't have
sufficient power.

00:40:24.641 --> 00:40:27.010
So, I'm going to move next
to the supermarket data,

00:40:27.011 --> 00:40:29.925
where I have like thousands
of price changes and there,

00:40:29.926 --> 00:40:33.635
it's cleaner to sort
of pick that out.

00:40:33.636 --> 00:40:37.615
So, I think that the thing
on the distance though,

00:40:37.616 --> 00:40:39.435
is in some sense I'm seeing,

00:40:39.436 --> 00:40:42.230
I'm kind of generalizing
a little bit.

00:40:42.231 --> 00:40:45.210
There are some some extrapolation
assumptions basically,

00:40:45.211 --> 00:40:47.840
that I'm assuming that these,

00:40:47.841 --> 00:40:51.435
if I'm seeing users who
have different choice sets,

00:40:51.436 --> 00:40:54.740
then I can infer something
about distance and

00:40:54.741 --> 00:40:56.490
also the fact that I'm
seeing this user go to

00:40:56.491 --> 00:40:58.520
lots of different restaurants.

00:40:58.521 --> 00:41:00.600
But I am relying at some level on

00:41:00.601 --> 00:41:02.820
functional form assumptions
to tease out how to

00:41:02.821 --> 00:41:07.910
attribute things to distance
versus Chinese versus price.

00:41:07.911 --> 00:41:09.640
So, I'm learning that partly by

00:41:09.641 --> 00:41:12.395
looking across users. Yeah.

00:41:12.396 --> 00:41:15.380
&gt;&gt; One thing for
the highest quintile that users,

00:41:15.381 --> 00:41:18.930
I mean that L last seems
to like drop down here?

00:41:18.931 --> 00:41:21.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's,
it's a little weird.

00:41:21.531 --> 00:41:22.990
I don't know about that one.

00:41:22.991 --> 00:41:28.470
With the price range, we
don't have any data but here,

00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:33.400
again this could be
an cautionary tale that

00:41:33.401 --> 00:41:35.860
you shouldn't interpret
that structurally.

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:40.765
Okay. So, we also look at
differences across cities,

00:41:40.766 --> 00:41:44.180
and we see that
there're some cities

00:41:44.181 --> 00:41:45.930
where people are willing
to travel far and

00:41:45.931 --> 00:41:48.240
these tend to be the ones where
things are farther apart.

00:41:48.241 --> 00:41:50.140
While there're less
willing to travel in

00:41:50.141 --> 00:41:53.070
some of the denser cities.

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:57.450
Then, we look at how the
model predictions for

00:41:57.451 --> 00:41:59.914
the effects of restaurant
openings and closings

00:41:59.915 --> 00:42:01.800
controlling for other changes.

00:42:01.801 --> 00:42:04.855
So, we look at if a
restaurant closed,

00:42:04.856 --> 00:42:06.220
what is the impact on

00:42:06.221 --> 00:42:09.505
the typical restaurant
within two miles,

00:42:09.506 --> 00:42:12.070
two to four miles, four
to six miles, and so on.

00:42:12.071 --> 00:42:14.720
So, this model basically tells us

00:42:14.721 --> 00:42:16.280
how the impact of
the restaurant opening

00:42:16.281 --> 00:42:17.905
and closing is distributed.

00:42:17.906 --> 00:42:21.830
So, another problem with
this application as it is now,

00:42:21.831 --> 00:42:23.505
and so I'm like,

00:42:23.506 --> 00:42:25.500
by the way you've probably
figured out by now I

00:42:25.501 --> 00:42:27.310
don't like go to town
defending my papers.

00:42:27.311 --> 00:42:29.310
So, I'm telling you
their strengths and weaknesses.

00:42:29.311 --> 00:42:32.330
But we're trying to extend
this in a variety of ways.

00:42:32.331 --> 00:42:35.100
The thing that actually varies
in the data as we do see

00:42:35.101 --> 00:42:37.960
restaurants opening and
closing, but each restaurant.

00:42:37.961 --> 00:42:40.060
There's so many restaurants
that each restaurant has

00:42:40.061 --> 00:42:43.290
only this very tiny impact on
the restaurants around it.

00:42:43.291 --> 00:42:46.835
So, you need a lot of data
to actually pin this down.

00:42:46.836 --> 00:42:48.800
So, in particular here,

00:42:48.801 --> 00:42:52.050
these are the actual impacts
from the restaurants.

00:42:52.051 --> 00:42:54.790
We have several 100 held-out
restaurants openings

00:42:54.791 --> 00:42:56.495
and closings in the data.

00:42:56.496 --> 00:42:59.180
But we see that
the standard deviations are

00:42:59.181 --> 00:43:01.720
really big for the actuals,

00:43:01.721 --> 00:43:03.040
and so you're not
really going to be

00:43:03.041 --> 00:43:04.620
able to tell that your model has

00:43:04.621 --> 00:43:08.190
performed well by how well
it fits these actuals.

00:43:08.191 --> 00:43:10.640
So, this was a proof
of concept paper,

00:43:10.641 --> 00:43:13.100
and so now we get a sense
of the power calculation.

00:43:13.101 --> 00:43:15.835
We need to do
many more cities and

00:43:15.836 --> 00:43:18.910
a longer time period in order
to get these error bars

00:43:18.911 --> 00:43:20.610
down to be a meaningful level

00:43:20.611 --> 00:43:22.230
where we could say one model is

00:43:22.231 --> 00:43:23.860
better than another model at

00:43:23.861 --> 00:43:26.070
doing those
