out out of stock data at
approximately the hourly level,

00:53:54.321 --> 00:53:57.430
so they could figure out if
a product when out of stock.

00:53:57.431 --> 00:53:58.810
We also in the store,

00:53:58.811 --> 00:54:01.165
all the prices changed
on Tuesday night.

00:54:01.166 --> 00:54:03.790
So, and the nice thing
about Tuesday night,

00:54:03.791 --> 00:54:05.050
and I think this is partly
why they picked it,

00:54:05.051 --> 00:54:06.150
is that Tuesday and Wednesday are

00:54:06.151 --> 00:54:07.655
very similar shopping days.

00:54:07.656 --> 00:54:10.215
They're indistinguishable in
terms of what people buy.

00:54:10.216 --> 00:54:12.290
Friday is different,
Monday is different,

00:54:12.291 --> 00:54:14.085
nighttime is different
than daytime

00:54:14.086 --> 00:54:15.335
even for the same consumer.

00:54:15.336 --> 00:54:16.970
But roughly Tuesday
and Wednesday are

00:54:16.971 --> 00:54:20.295
very similar days in
terms of how people shop.

00:54:20.296 --> 00:54:23.215
Then we have the product
hierarchy for this.

00:54:23.216 --> 00:54:24.530
So, in the first paper
we're going to

00:54:24.531 --> 00:54:26.400
use the observed
product hierarchy.

00:54:26.401 --> 00:54:27.720
So, I know the UPC and

00:54:27.721 --> 00:54:30.555
all these different
categorizations of the product.

00:54:30.556 --> 00:54:34.100
In the second paper
on the same dataset,

00:54:34.101 --> 00:54:36.170
I'm going to throw this away
and I'm going to try to

00:54:36.171 --> 00:54:40.100
infer those things from
the choice behavior.

00:54:40.101 --> 00:54:42.280
So, we look at users who had

00:54:42.281 --> 00:54:43.980
at least 20 shopping trips
on Tuesday or

00:54:43.981 --> 00:54:45.735
Wednesday with
more than 10 items per trip.

00:54:45.736 --> 00:54:48.710
We started with
the top 235 categories.

00:54:48.711 --> 00:54:50.200
So, a category will
be like tomatoes

00:54:50.201 --> 00:54:52.435
or toilet paper or yogurt.

00:54:52.436 --> 00:54:54.580
We throw out things

00:54:54.581 --> 00:54:57.315
where people basically
only bought one product.

00:54:57.316 --> 00:54:59.660
There's somethings like
frozen dinners where all of

00:54:59.661 --> 00:55:00.880
the products in the category

00:55:00.881 --> 00:55:02.320
have prices that move together.

00:55:02.321 --> 00:55:04.450
It's like all the frozen
dinners are expensive

00:55:04.451 --> 00:55:05.640
one week and they're cheap

00:55:05.641 --> 00:55:06.950
the next week. So,
we threw those out.

00:55:06.951 --> 00:55:08.420
So, we only kept categories where

00:55:08.421 --> 00:55:10.090
people bought
different products from

00:55:10.091 --> 00:55:11.860
the categories and where

00:55:11.861 --> 00:55:13.865
there was independent
variation in the price,

00:55:13.866 --> 00:55:16.020
because here now I'm going to
try to be a little bit more

00:55:16.021 --> 00:55:19.790
rigorous on the causal
identification.

00:55:19.791 --> 00:55:22.180
So, I ended up with
2,000 consumers,

00:55:22.181 --> 00:55:25.240
123 categories, 1,263 items,

00:55:25.241 --> 00:55:30.590
and 333,000 trips and rather
than do cross-validation,

00:55:30.591 --> 00:55:32.210
because the models are
expensive to compute,

00:55:32.211 --> 00:55:35.185
we just have a single
validation set. Yap.

00:55:35.186 --> 00:55:38.130
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] How are
the prices change?

00:55:38.131 --> 00:55:40.170
&gt;&gt; Who changes them?
Or what are they

00:55:40.171 --> 00:55:41.780
thinking about when
they change them?

00:55:41.781 --> 00:55:44.270
Yeah, that's a
little complicated.

00:55:44.271 --> 00:55:47.070
It can be because
manufacturer costs change

00:55:47.071 --> 00:55:48.750
sometimes or it could be because

00:55:48.751 --> 00:55:50.540
the store decided to do it.

00:55:50.541 --> 00:55:54.825
Ultimately the chain often
decides at the chain level.

00:55:54.826 --> 00:55:56.590
But something you might
be concerned about

00:55:56.591 --> 00:55:58.630
is on some weeks,

00:55:58.631 --> 00:56:00.180
going from Tuesday
to Wednesday you

00:56:00.181 --> 00:56:03.890
might raise the price because
Thursday is Thanksgiving,

00:56:03.891 --> 00:56:06.130
and so we're going to do some
testing and actually throw

00:56:06.131 --> 00:56:08.750
out products that have
that kind of a problem.

00:56:08.751 --> 00:56:11.655
So, let me show you how we
do the testing for that.

00:56:11.656 --> 00:56:12.990
So, this is a price series,

00:56:12.991 --> 00:56:16.945
this is a typical price series
for a single UPC.

00:56:16.946 --> 00:56:19.050
This is what a price series
looks like for

00:56:19.051 --> 00:56:22.550
the average prices for a
category mainstream white bread.

00:56:22.551 --> 00:56:26.580
So, we do see that there can
be like systematic trends in

00:56:26.581 --> 00:56:30.190
those prices but there's also

00:56:30.191 --> 00:56:31.680
like once you look across

00:56:31.681 --> 00:56:32.580
all the prices there's actually

00:56:32.581 --> 00:56:33.940
a fair bit of price variation.

00:56:33.941 --> 00:56:36.350
In most categories there's
a price change most weeks.

00:56:36.351 --> 00:56:38.070
So, we do actually have
a lot of if you think of

00:56:38.071 --> 00:56:39.925
each price change
as an experiment,

00:56:39.926 --> 00:56:41.795
we have a lot of experiments.

00:56:41.796 --> 00:56:45.000
If we want to think about
identifying assumption,

00:56:45.001 --> 00:56:46.270
we're basically going to say that

00:56:46.271 --> 00:56:47.830
the counterfactual
distribution of

00:56:47.831 --> 00:56:49.110
purchases between Tuesday and

00:56:49.111 --> 00:56:51.610
Wednesday in weeks with
price changes can be

00:56:51.611 --> 00:56:53.370
constructed from their behavior

00:56:53.371 --> 00:56:55.080
on the weeks without
price changes.

00:56:55.081 --> 00:56:56.350
So, what we're going to do

00:56:56.351 --> 00:56:57.570
is we're basically
going to control,

00:56:57.571 --> 00:57:00.040
we're going to have dummies
for each week and category.

00:57:00.041 --> 00:57:01.985
So, we're basically
going to learn

00:57:01.986 --> 00:57:04.160
how popular this product
is this week or

00:57:04.161 --> 00:57:06.400
this category is in this week
and then we're also

00:57:06.401 --> 00:57:08.660
going to learn how different
Tuesday and Wednesday are.

00:57:08.661 --> 00:57:10.650
It turns out they're not
actually different so that's not

00:57:10.651 --> 00:57:12.990
very important but we
control for that anyways.

00:57:12.991 --> 00:57:14.400
So, essentially
what we're going to

00:57:14.401 --> 00:57:16.440
assume is that if I tell

00:57:16.441 --> 00:57:21.375
you how popular this week

00:57:21.376 --> 00:57:24.080
is and I tell you whether
to Tuesday or Wednesday,

00:57:24.081 --> 00:57:25.950
I can figure out what would have

00:57:25.951 --> 00:57:28.485
happened if the price
hadn't changed.

00:57:28.486 --> 00:57:31.455
So, that's a functional
form assumption,

00:57:31.456 --> 00:57:34.625
but we think it's a pretty
good one in the sense that

00:57:34.626 --> 00:57:36.090
in most products in most weeks

00:57:36.091 --> 00:57:38.040
Tuesday and Wednesday or
pretty much the same.

00:57:38.041 --> 00:57:39.660
But, we're not going
to just accept that,

00:57:39.661 --> 00:57:41.480
we're going to do what
we call a Placebo test.

00:57:41.481 --> 00:57:44.395
Now, this is not a definitive
test, it's just suggestive.

00:57:44.396 --> 00:57:46.030
But it maybe provide you

00:57:46.031 --> 00:57:47.110
some competence and it also

00:57:47.111 --> 00:57:48.330
gives you a way to
kick things out.

00:57:48.331 --> 00:57:50.820
So, we use this to kick out
stone fruits for example.

00:57:50.821 --> 00:57:52.760
Turns out peaches are
really seasonal and

00:57:52.761 --> 00:57:53.940
so there's some things

00:57:53.941 --> 00:57:55.705
that flunk the test
I'm going to do.

00:57:55.706 --> 00:57:58.500
So, what we're going to do
is create fake price series,

00:57:58.501 --> 00:57:59.745
we're going to shift
the price series

00:57:59.746 --> 00:58:01.455
forward or back in time,

00:58:01.456 --> 00:58:04.230
and then we're going to estimate

00:58:04.231 --> 00:58:06.275
the model with fake price series,

00:58:06.276 --> 00:58:08.330
and if there was
an ongoing trend, so,

00:58:08.331 --> 00:58:09.930
the prices are all
going up and also

00:58:09.931 --> 00:58:11.925
demand is going up
at the same time,

00:58:11.926 --> 00:58:13.290
then it would look like there was

00:58:13.291 --> 00:58:14.410
a price change even in weeks

00:58:14.411 --> 00:58:15.730
where there weren't
a price change.

00:58:15.731 --> 00:58:17.120
So, we're testing for

00:58:17.121 --> 00:58:19.245
a certain type of
problem in our model.

00:58:19.246 --> 00:58:20.640
What we do is we estimate

00:58:20.641 --> 00:58:23.200
a simple multinomial logit model

00:58:23.201 --> 00:58:25.600
and we get the coefficients
on price and we

00:58:25.601 --> 00:58:27.070
want to look at this is what

00:58:27.071 --> 00:58:28.950
the price shifts look like
and we want to look at

00:58:28.951 --> 00:58:30.855
the distribution of the P-values

00:58:30.856 --> 00:58:32.785
on the price coefficient.

00:58:32.786 --> 00:58:34.710
So, in particular, if

00:58:34.711 --> 00:58:38.070
these prices were
just random noise,

00:58:38.071 --> 00:58:39.530
then the P-values on

00:58:39.531 --> 00:58:42.035
the price coefficient should
be uniformly distributed.

00:58:42.036 --> 00:58:45.930
There should be no effect
of a noisy price series,

00:58:45.931 --> 00:58:49.445
and so we should see a uniform
distribution of P-values.

00:58:49.446 --> 00:58:51.880
This red is the
actual distribution

00:58:51.881 --> 00:58:54.080
of P-values with
the actual price series,

00:58:54.081 --> 00:58:56.505
which is saying that
most of the time

00:58:56.506 --> 00:58:58.430
we think that there's

00:58:58.431 --> 00:59:00.910
a significantly negative
effect of the price.

00:59:00.911 --> 00:59:02.955
But with the fake price series,

00:59:02.956 --> 00:59:06.320
they look roughly uniform
although not perfectly uniform,

00:59:06.321 --> 00:59:08.420
and we're arguing
that this sort of

00:59:08.421 --> 00:59:11.805
validates that are given
all the controls we put in

00:59:11.806 --> 00:59:15.850
that the prices that our
assumption is good and then if

00:59:15.851 --> 00:59:18.060
not there's some other time trend

00:59:18.061 --> 00:59:20.775
going on that we haven't
captured in our model.

00:59:20.776 --> 00:59:23.010
We're not testing
every alternative,

00:59:23.011 --> 00:59:26.060
we're just testing alternatives
about gradual shifts,

00:59:26.061 --> 00:59:28.790
up or gradual shifts down
in a product's popularity.

00:59:28.791 --> 00:59:31.500
So, I'd say this type
of Placebo test

00:59:31.501 --> 00:59:34.950
is been used in economics
but not usually it's scale,

00:59:34.951 --> 00:59:36.870
like we usually do
much simpler versions of

00:59:36.871 --> 00:59:38.070
these and I would
say that there's

00:59:38.071 --> 00:59:39.440
some interesting ideas of

00:59:39.441 --> 00:59:41.460
bringing these kinds of
tests into machine learning

00:59:41.461 --> 00:59:44.965
settings where we have
lots of experiments,

00:59:44.966 --> 00:59:47.570
and that idea I think we do

00:59:47.571 --> 00:59:50.360
it in a very small
hand tuning way.

00:59:50.361 --> 00:59:54.295
Suppose there was
a minimum wage change in 1984,

00:59:54.296 --> 00:59:56.070
we see or would we think
that the minimum wage would

00:59:56.071 --> 00:59:58.230
change in 1983 if the model

00:59:58.231 --> 01:00:00.340
says that it looks like there was

01:00:00.341 --> 01:00:01.390
an effect of a change that didn't

01:00:01.391 --> 01:00:02.530
happen then we reject our model.

01:00:02.531 --> 01:00:04.120
But it's usually one change in

01:00:04.121 --> 01:00:06.560
one year not lots
of little changes.

01:00:06.561 --> 01:00:08.210
Okay. So, if we say we

01:00:08.211 --> 01:00:09.890
throw out some things
and this works,

01:00:09.891 --> 01:00:14.875
then let me go on to our model.

01:00:14.876 --> 01:00:17.040
So, our model like before,

01:00:17.041 --> 01:00:18.300
we're going to have
user utilities,

01:00:18.301 --> 01:00:20.380
they're going to have
factorized mean utilities.

01:00:20.381 --> 01:00:22.165
Now instead of
distance we have price

01:00:22.166 --> 01:00:24.565
and we're going to have
factorized price coefficients.

01:00:24.566 --> 01:00:26.770
Again, this model is going to

01:00:26.771 --> 01:00:29.955
say here conditional on
purchasing in a category,

01:00:29.956 --> 01:00:31.950
you have a multinomial
logit form.

01:00:31.951 --> 01:00:33.300
But we're we're going to add to

01:00:33.301 --> 01:00:36.020
our previous model is
the nested logit formulation,

01:00:36.021 --> 01:00:37.780
which has proved
to be very popular

01:00:37.781 --> 01:00:40.195
and fits very well in economics.

01:00:40.196 --> 01:00:42.390
So, the problem is
that if I included

01:00:42.391 --> 01:00:43.910
the outside good
which is not buying

01:00:43.911 --> 01:00:45.630
anything in this model,

01:00:45.631 --> 01:00:47.180
the assumption that
the errors are

01:00:47.181 --> 01:00:49.175
IID is actually
a really bad assumption,

01:00:49.176 --> 01:00:50.890
because some days you're
going to go to the store

01:00:50.891 --> 01:00:52.535
and you really need paper
towels and so there's

01:00:52.536 --> 01:00:55.770
a correlated positive shock
for all the paper towels and

01:00:55.771 --> 01:00:59.290
not buying a paper towel
is a separate option.

01:00:59.291 --> 01:01:00.550
So what the nested logit

01:01:00.551 --> 01:01:02.460
basically assumes
is that first you

01:01:02.461 --> 01:01:04.180
choose buy a product or not buy

01:01:04.181 --> 01:01:05.310
a product and then you choose

01:01:05.311 --> 01:01:06.840
what to buy in the category.

01:01:06.841 --> 01:01:09.130
And technically, it
allows for correlation in

01:01:09.131 --> 01:01:11.820
these errors across
all the products

01:01:11.821 --> 01:01:13.140
other than don't buy.

01:01:13.141 --> 01:01:15.380
But a certain form
of correlation,

01:01:15.381 --> 01:01:16.260
and the nice thing about

01:01:16.261 --> 01:01:17.460
the nested logic
from a computational

01:01:17.461 --> 01:01:18.790
perspective is that
you can actually

01:01:18.791 --> 01:01:23.335
estimate it as is two
sequential multinomial logits.

01:01:23.336 --> 01:01:26.220
So, it's more efficient
to jointly estimate them.

01:01:26.221 --> 01:01:28.360
So, likes data in R, will
jointly estimate them,

01:01:28.361 --> 01:01:28.910
but because I have

01:01:28.911 --> 01:01:31.170
a very complicated
computational problem

01:01:31.171 --> 01:01:32.335
with all my latent variables,

01:01:32.336 --> 01:01:34.470
I'm going to estimate
them sequentially which

01:01:34.471 --> 01:01:38.110
loses something but it works.

01:01:38.111 --> 01:01:40.495
So, first, I'm going to model

01:01:40.496 --> 01:01:42.420
this multinomial logit with

01:01:42.421 --> 01:01:44.890
all my latent variables
and then I'm going to

01:01:44.891 --> 01:01:47.865
model the choice of whether
to buy anything at all.

01:01:47.866 --> 01:01:49.390
There's going to
be a factorization

01:01:49.391 --> 01:01:51.150
of the choice, Don't
buy anything at all.

01:01:51.151 --> 01:01:53.080
So, it's a proof,

01:01:53.081 --> 01:01:54.855
it's like a page of algebra.

01:01:54.856 --> 01:01:59.390
You can show that if you put in

01:01:59.391 --> 01:02:04.580
the utilities for all of
the other items in the category,

01:02:04.581 --> 01:02:06.115
that's called the
inclusive value,

01:02:06.116 --> 01:02:08.320
using this crazy
functional form here,

01:02:08.321 --> 01:02:11.320
inclusive value is log of
the sum of e to the Us,

01:02:11.321 --> 01:02:13.700
then you will get

01:02:13.701 --> 01:02:15.755
the parameters of
the user's utility function.

01:02:15.756 --> 01:02:17.420
So, I won't show you
that proof but that was

01:02:17.421 --> 01:02:19.000
part of [inaudible] work in

01:02:19.001 --> 01:02:20.300
the early 70s was to do

01:02:20.301 --> 01:02:21.390
this algebra which turned

01:02:21.391 --> 01:02:22.740
out to be very
convenient algebra.

01:02:22.741 --> 01:02:25.260
So, now, I'm going to do
a two-stage estimation.

01:02:25.261 --> 01:02:26.510
First, I'll do this model,

01:02:26.511 --> 01:02:27.545
get all the parameters,

01:02:27.546 --> 01:02:28.830
figure out the utilities,

01:02:28.831 --> 01:02:30.250
then I take the utilities,

01:02:30.251 --> 01:02:33.130
construct the inclusive value
of just buying something,

01:02:33.131 --> 01:02:35.860
and then I can do
another multinomial logit

01:02:35.861 --> 01:02:39.225
to choose between buying
something and buying nothing.

01:02:39.226 --> 01:02:42.040
That allows for
the correlation and it's

01:02:42.041 --> 01:02:43.100
very important for fitting

01:02:43.101 --> 01:02:45.110
shopping data because
most consumers don't purchase,

01:02:45.111 --> 01:02:47.160
so those zeros are really
important in the data,

01:02:47.161 --> 01:02:48.470
and this is something
other people have

01:02:48.471 --> 01:02:50.180
observed in machine
learning as well,

01:02:50.181 --> 01:02:51.770
trying to model the zeros is

01:02:51.771 --> 01:02:54.015
really important for
modeling trace data.

01:02:54.016 --> 01:02:56.675
Okay, we also use
Bayesian inference again,

01:02:56.676 --> 01:02:59.755
and so, I'm just here to
show you a few things,

01:02:59.756 --> 01:03:02.350
this actually was
just a total pain in the ass,

01:03:02.351 --> 01:03:03.920
but we compared a zillion

01:03:03.921 --> 01:03:05.770
different common economic models

01:03:05.771 --> 01:03:06.800
and we win,

01:03:06.801 --> 01:03:09.050
otherwise I wouldn't be
showing you the slide.

01:03:09.051 --> 01:03:13.450
So, the thing that's more
interesting, I would say,

01:03:13.451 --> 01:03:16.125
that we found is that we actually

01:03:16.126 --> 01:03:20.340
focus on how we do in
terms of counterfactuals.

01:03:20.341 --> 01:03:22.510
This is all weeks,
this is weeks where

01:03:22.511 --> 01:03:24.880
one of the products in the
category is out of stock,

01:03:24.881 --> 01:03:26.270
this is weeks where one of

01:03:26.271 --> 01:03:28.000
the products has a price change,

01:03:28.001 --> 01:03:29.090
and I'm only looking at

01:03:29.091 --> 01:03:31.425
your purchases of the product
that had a price change,

01:03:31.426 --> 01:03:33.770
and this is observations

01:03:33.771 --> 01:03:36.480
where another product in the
category had a price change,

01:03:36.481 --> 01:03:37.650
and I'm looking at purchases of

01:03:37.651 --> 01:03:39.590
the product that didn't
have the price change.

01:03:39.591 --> 01:03:42.250
So, these are all
different counterfactuals,

01:03:42.251 --> 01:03:44.380
and so we actually end up finding

01:03:44.381 --> 01:03:47.190
different model selection
if we tune for,

01:03:47.191 --> 01:03:51.040
say, the price change
counterfactual rather than overall.

01:03:51.041 --> 01:03:55.750
So, it's important to tune
your model for your goal and

01:03:55.751 --> 01:03:58.100
counterfactual will put more
of a weight on trying to

01:03:58.101 --> 01:04:00.585
understand the role of
the price changes while,

01:04:00.586 --> 01:04:01.750
otherwise, you might put

01:04:01.751 --> 01:04:04.590
more role work into
the mean utility.

01:04:04.591 --> 01:04:08.635
So then, some of the things
that we get out of that,

01:04:08.636 --> 01:04:11.660
we used data about

01:04:11.661 --> 01:04:13.210
categories but we
didn't use about

01:04:13.211 --> 01:04:15.560
subcategories which are
called classes or subclasses.

01:04:15.561 --> 01:04:16.845
So, within water, there's

01:04:16.846 --> 01:04:18.290
sparkling water and then there's

01:04:18.291 --> 01:04:19.700
flavored sparkling water is

01:04:19.701 --> 01:04:22.365
a subclass, that's
just an example.

01:04:22.366 --> 01:04:25.580
So, what we find is
that our model does

01:04:25.581 --> 01:04:29.515
a better job at understanding
what's in the same class.

01:04:29.516 --> 01:04:33.610
So, we find much higher
cross-price elasticity,

01:04:33.611 --> 01:04:35.490
so the impact of
one product's price on

01:04:35.491 --> 01:04:38.765
another product within
a class than across classes.

01:04:38.766 --> 01:04:40.025
So, we understand that

01:04:40.026 --> 01:04:41.960
sparkling waters are
better substitutes

01:04:41.961 --> 01:04:43.060
than flat water and

01:04:43.061 --> 01:04:46.475
sparkling water in a way
that the other models can't.

01:04:46.476 --> 01:04:50.975
Okay. So, it's not
quite done yet,

01:04:50.976 --> 01:04:52.170
this is the first project,

01:04:52.171 --> 01:04:54.075
we started this three years ago,

01:04:54.076 --> 01:04:55.620
but we have continued.

01:04:55.621 --> 01:04:57.970
First, we had to make
all these changes,

01:04:57.971 --> 01:04:59.650
we had to add
the nested logit and

01:04:59.651 --> 01:05:01.290
this functional form and the way

01:05:01.291 --> 01:05:03.245
we brought in
the co-variants and so on.

01:05:03.246 --> 01:05:06.430
Then now, what we're
finishing is doing

01:05:06.431 --> 01:05:10.195
personalized pricing and
couponing strategies using this.

01:05:10.196 --> 01:05:13.595
Relative to the
economics literature,

01:05:13.596 --> 01:05:15.620
we spend a lot of time showing

01:05:15.621 --> 01:05:17.835
how pooling information
across users and

01:05:17.836 --> 01:05:20.230
items really adds a lot of value.

01:05:20.231 --> 01:05:22.210
So, one of the things,
if I just study lettuce,

01:05:22.211 --> 01:05:23.510
you don't buy lettuce
that much and there's

01:05:23.511 --> 01:05:24.990
not many price
changes for lettuce.

01:05:24.991 --> 01:05:25.930
But if I understand that

01:05:25.931 --> 01:05:27.160
your price sensitivity
for lettuce

01:05:27.161 --> 01:05:30.070
is similar to your price
sensitivity for tomatoes,

01:05:30.071 --> 01:05:31.570
and I understand
that tomatoes and

01:05:31.571 --> 01:05:33.620
lettuce have
common latent factors,

01:05:33.621 --> 01:05:36.390
then even if I don't see
you buy tomatoes very much,

01:05:36.391 --> 01:05:38.070
I can learn about
your price sensitivity for

01:05:38.071 --> 01:05:40.590
tomatoes from your price
sensitivity for lettuce.

01:05:40.591 --> 01:05:44.230
So that cross-product learning
allows you to get a lot

01:05:44.231 --> 01:05:45.955
more out of a dataset

01:05:45.956 --> 01:05:47.735
where people don't purchase
things very often.

01:05:47.736 --> 01:05:49.480
So, most consumers
purchase most products,

01:05:49.481 --> 01:05:50.950
like, one percent of the time,

01:05:50.951 --> 01:05:52.150
so I think that's
going to be also like

01:05:52.151 --> 01:05:54.660
a common theme if you
think about other types of

01:05:54.661 --> 01:05:56.680
browsing and online data where

01:05:56.681 --> 01:05:59.490
most consumers don't do
most things very often.

01:05:59.491 --> 01:06:01.780
Now, at some level, that insight,

01:06:01.781 --> 01:06:04.320
you guys already knew that
because that's the insight of

01:06:04.321 --> 01:06:05.575
the Netflix composition and

01:06:05.576 --> 01:06:07.085
matrix factorization so you're,

01:06:07.086 --> 01:06:09.275
like, "What took you so long?"

01:06:09.276 --> 01:06:10.835
In economics, we didn't.

01:06:10.836 --> 01:06:14.690
Nobody tried it so we're
showing them that that works.

01:06:14.691 --> 01:06:17.070
So, I think the benefit relative

01:06:17.071 --> 01:06:18.135
to the machine learning
literature for

01:06:18.136 --> 01:06:19.520
this is that actually,

01:06:19.521 --> 01:06:22.245
there were a large number

01:06:22.246 --> 01:06:23.910
of computational tweaks we needed

01:06:23.911 --> 01:06:25.600
to get to get this
to run at scale.

01:06:25.601 --> 01:06:27.640
Once you put in
the multinomial logit form,

01:06:27.641 --> 01:06:28.700
we have the softmax and

01:06:28.701 --> 01:06:31.145
then non-linear and we have
these structural parameters,

01:06:31.146 --> 01:06:33.340
so that was the part of
the computational thing,

01:06:33.341 --> 01:06:34.250
really, embedding it in

01:06:34.251 --> 01:06:36.385
the utility
maximization framework.

01:06:36.386 --> 01:06:40.250
Now, I want to talk
about the last paper

01:06:40.251 --> 01:06:41.260
which in some sense is

01:06:41.261 --> 01:06:43.955
the most innovative
computationally,

01:06:43.956 --> 01:06:45.660
although I'll go back to

01:06:45.661 --> 01:06:47.380
a little bit of apologizing
because I would

01:06:47.381 --> 01:06:50.670
say that the model is good
but it's not the final model.

01:06:50.671 --> 01:06:52.065
So, in this paper,

01:06:52.066 --> 01:06:53.960
we've got the same dataset,

01:06:53.961 --> 01:06:55.710
basically the same problem,

01:06:55.711 --> 01:06:59.295
but now I want to throw away
my item information and say,

01:06:59.296 --> 01:07:02.250
"I don't know how these products
relate to each other,

01:07:02.251 --> 01:07:03.780
and further, I want to

01:07:03.781 --> 01:07:05.929
allow that some of them
are actually complements."

01:07:05.930 --> 01:07:08.080
So, the old model
said, "Paper, towels,

01:07:08.081 --> 01:07:09.250
and tomatoes are just completely

01:07:09.251 --> 01:07:10.840
separate. They don't
affect each other."

01:07:10.841 --> 01:07:12.200
Then we further
assumed you're only

01:07:12.201 --> 01:07:13.565
going to choose one paper towel,

01:07:13.566 --> 01:07:16.225
you're only going to
choose one kind of tomato,

01:07:16.226 --> 01:07:19.190
and we threw away things
where you bought a lot,

01:07:19.191 --> 01:07:20.200
like multiple varieties of

01:07:20.201 --> 01:07:21.885
yogurt, we actually
threw away yogurt.

01:07:21.886 --> 01:07:24.490
Now, we're going to
ask a question like

01:07:24.491 --> 01:07:26.820
"Can I figure out that
hotdogs and buns go together?

01:07:26.821 --> 01:07:29.305
Actually, could I
figure out that even

01:07:29.306 --> 01:07:32.460
though water and juice are
in different categories,

01:07:32.461 --> 01:07:35.100
those are actually also
imperfect substitutes.

01:07:35.101 --> 01:07:37.245
Can I learn all of
that from the data?"

01:07:37.246 --> 01:07:39.970
So, I actually started
working on this problem in

01:07:39.971 --> 01:07:43.320
1995 and I solved it for

01:07:43.321 --> 01:07:44.880
two choices and then we

01:07:44.881 --> 01:07:47.095
gave up because we
weren't able to go

01:07:47.096 --> 01:07:50.910
beyond three or four
computationally in the 90s.

01:07:50.911 --> 01:07:52.930
Of course, if I had met all
of you guys in the 90s,

01:07:52.931 --> 01:07:55.800
there were those of you who
are still alive as scholars,

01:07:55.801 --> 01:07:57.840
you probably could have
helped me at that time

01:07:57.841 --> 01:08:03.255
but you weren't born yet
as scholars in the 90s.

01:08:03.256 --> 01:08:06.770
But it was still a hard problem.

01:08:06.771 --> 01:08:09.670
So, we studied this and
that the paper came out,

01:08:09.671 --> 01:08:12.400
it's actually
a pretty well-cited paper,

01:08:12.401 --> 01:08:16.175
but we stopped with
three or four choices.

01:08:16.176 --> 01:08:18.180
So, let me actually just
skip over that picture,

01:08:18.181 --> 01:08:19.370
I'll just tell you that there was

01:08:19.371 --> 01:08:21.210
a very small [inaudible]

01:08:21.211 --> 01:08:23.560
listed every economics paper
that's ever tried

01:08:23.561 --> 01:08:26.090
to estimate complementarity
between two or three things

01:08:26.091 --> 01:08:29.000
and they've maxed out
at three roughly.

01:08:29.001 --> 01:08:32.940
So, I've got three choices
and I'm going to see,

01:08:32.941 --> 01:08:35.330
basically there's
two to the three,

01:08:35.331 --> 01:08:37.480
there's eight bundles,
and I'm going to try

01:08:37.481 --> 01:08:39.735
to understand your preferences
for those eight bundles.

01:08:39.736 --> 01:08:40.800
But of course, these things get

01:08:40.801 --> 01:08:42.245
very complicated very fast.

01:08:42.246 --> 01:08:46.349
If I wanted to go back and
think I have 5,000 choices,

01:08:46.350 --> 01:08:47.755
there's a question of, like,

01:08:47.756 --> 01:08:48.975
"Well, wait a second.

01:08:48.976 --> 01:08:50.820
Not only is this
hard to compute for

01:08:50.821 --> 01:08:53.740
me but how the heck could
the consumer do it?"

01:08:53.741 --> 01:08:56.320
I'm using a utility
maximization framework and I'm

01:08:56.321 --> 01:08:58.660
trying to infer what
the consumer was doing.

01:08:58.661 --> 01:09:01.770
When they maximized, there
were two to the 5,000 bundles,

01:09:01.771 --> 01:09:03.445
but isn't that idiotic?

01:09:03.446 --> 01:09:04.730
At some level, right?

01:09:04.731 --> 01:09:06.220
They can't really do that.

01:09:06.221 --> 01:09:08.180
So, the approach
we're going to take

01:09:08.181 --> 01:09:10.370
in this paper is
we're going to impose

01:09:10.371 --> 01:09:12.700
an arbitrary heuristic on how

01:09:12.701 --> 01:09:14.740
the consumers shop
and then try to

01:09:14.741 --> 01:09:17.525
use that and estimate the
parameters of that heuristic.

01:09:17.526 --> 01:09:19.060
The problem with that is why my

01:09:19.061 --> 01:09:20.855
heuristic and not somebody
else's heuristic.

01:09:20.856 --> 01:09:23.380
I would say, "Yeah,
that's a problem."

01:09:23.381 --> 01:09:26.510
But I'm going to see if I get
sensible things out of it.

01:09:26.511 --> 01:09:28.410
Okay. So, what we're going to do

01:09:28.411 --> 01:09:30.710
is we're going to make
a behavioral assumption.

01:09:30.711 --> 01:09:34.275
So, when I say this, this
is not the last version,

01:09:34.276 --> 01:09:35.370
we have a bunch of

01:09:35.371 --> 01:09:37.685
extensions that are
in progress on this.

01:09:37.686 --> 01:09:39.430
But the basic behavioral
assumption is

01:09:39.431 --> 01:09:41.420
a myopic consumer
goes into the store,

01:09:41.421 --> 01:09:44.065
considers sequentially
what to buy,

01:09:44.066 --> 01:09:47.520
and they're going to go in
order of importance basically.

01:09:47.521 --> 01:09:49.350
So, we're going to start with
the thing that's highest

01:09:49.351 --> 01:09:51.745
in their utility from
everything in the store.

01:09:51.746 --> 01:09:54.080
They're going to put
that into their basket

01:09:54.081 --> 01:09:55.530
and then they're going
to say, "All right.

01:09:55.531 --> 01:09:56.850
What should I do next?"

01:09:56.851 --> 01:09:58.680
Then they're going to put
the next thing in the basket.

01:09:58.681 --> 01:10:00.745
But when they put
the next thing in the basket,

01:10:00.746 --> 01:10:02.865
they're going to consider
what's already in their basket.

01:10:02.866 --> 01:10:04.720
So, if they have
hotdog buns in the basket,

01:10:04.721 --> 01:10:07.260
they're going to consider
that when they buy hotdogs.

01:10:07.261 --> 01:10:09.635
Furthermore, there's going
to be a look-ahead model.

01:10:09.636 --> 01:10:12.265
So, the consumer is going to
look ahead to the next item.

01:10:12.266 --> 01:10:14.280
For now, I have them look
ahead to the whole store,

01:10:14.281 --> 01:10:15.580
although you can
imagine it's better for

01:10:15.581 --> 01:10:17.130
them to go sequentially
through sections of

01:10:17.131 --> 01:10:20.560
the store or to think about
things that go together.

01:10:20.561 --> 01:10:22.740
But anyways, they're going
to look right now and say,

01:10:22.741 --> 01:10:24.640
"I'm going to look ahead to
the next item accounting

01:10:24.641 --> 01:10:26.130
for the potential
complimentarity."

01:10:26.131 --> 01:10:27.120
Of course, you might want to look

01:10:27.121 --> 01:10:28.285
ahead two or three steps.

01:10:28.286 --> 01:10:29.895
Now, hopefully, a lot
of you are thinking,

01:10:29.896 --> 01:10:31.685
"Sounds like
reinforcement learning."

01:10:31.686 --> 01:10:33.640
Yeah, exactly. So, this
is fun and this is

01:10:33.641 --> 01:10:36.760
a connection that I'd
like to further develop.

01:10:36.761 --> 01:10:40.210
Okay. Then there's
a latent item ordering

01:10:40.211 --> 01:10:41.370
because they don't actually know

01:10:41.371 --> 01:10:43.035
what order they put
things in the basket,

01:10:43.036 --> 01:10:44.610
but this behavioral model

01:10:44.611 --> 01:10:46.890
implies a likelihood over orders,

01:10:46.891 --> 01:10:48.545
conditional on what
you purchased,

01:10:48.546 --> 01:10:50.000
there's all likelihood over that

01:10:50.001 --> 01:10:52.385
orders if you followed
that behavioral model.

01:10:52.386 --> 01:10:56.225
So, I can actually infer
that latent variable as well,

01:10:56.226 --> 01:10:58.240
and it's basically because

01:10:58.241 --> 01:11:00.770
higher value item should
have been purchased earlier.

01:11:00.771 --> 01:11:02.650
So, this is our model.

01:11:02.651 --> 01:11:05.500
So, we still have the same
functional form of utility,

01:11:05.501 --> 01:11:07.315
we're still going to
have logit errors,

01:11:07.316 --> 01:11:08.860
each end they make a choice.

01:11:08.861 --> 01:11:11.800
But now, we're assuming

01:11:11.801 --> 01:11:12.960
this is possible for

01:11:12.961 --> 01:11:14.805
complementarity in
this Look-Ahead Model.

01:11:14.806 --> 01:11:17.060
So, in the paper we write

01:11:17.061 --> 01:11:19.735
down what this means for
the Probabilistic Model.

01:11:19.736 --> 01:11:22.900
Okay. So, they just to get
a sense of how this works,

01:11:22.901 --> 01:11:24.330
this is simulated data.

01:11:24.331 --> 01:11:26.265
So, we're going to compare here

01:11:26.266 --> 01:11:28.700
a Myopic Model where you
just go to the store,

01:11:28.701 --> 01:11:31.350
you don't think ahead
to Think-Ahead model.

01:11:31.351 --> 01:11:33.140
We generated data about

01:11:33.141 --> 01:11:34.780
purchases from
all these products,

01:11:34.781 --> 01:11:36.550
and we generated them according

01:11:36.551 --> 01:11:38.350
to preferences that
are what you'd think.

01:11:38.351 --> 01:11:41.450
So, hotdogs and hotdog buns
are complements.

01:11:41.451 --> 01:11:44.010
What turns out in the data,
is it diapers and

01:11:44.011 --> 01:11:46.640
coffee are highly
positively correlated?

01:11:46.641 --> 01:11:48.660
I should pause there actually

01:11:48.661 --> 01:11:50.940
because that everybody
hears from economics.

01:11:50.941 --> 01:11:54.150
So, there's this distinction
between correlation and

01:11:54.151 --> 01:11:57.970
complementarity which we think
about a lot in economics,

01:11:57.971 --> 01:11:59.970
but maybe those words
are almost used

01:11:59.971 --> 01:12:02.945
interchangeably if you're not
used to thinking about it.

01:12:02.946 --> 01:12:06.635
So, we would say that coffee
and diapers are complements.

01:12:06.636 --> 01:12:09.349
If you lower the price of coffee,

01:12:09.350 --> 01:12:12.655
you would buy more coffee
and you'd buy more diapers,

01:12:12.656 --> 01:12:15.525
that means their compliments
because they go together.

01:12:15.526 --> 01:12:17.500
When you buy coffee
you want more diapers,

01:12:17.501 --> 01:12:20.190
when you buy diapers,
you buy more coffee.

01:12:20.191 --> 01:12:24.185
Similarly, if you raise
the price of coffee,

01:12:24.186 --> 01:12:26.070
you would buy less diapers.

01:12:26.071 --> 01:12:29.190
Okay. If they are correlated,

01:12:29.191 --> 01:12:31.530
it just means there might be
something about people that

01:12:31.531 --> 01:12:34.045
makes them like
coffee and diapers,

01:12:34.046 --> 01:12:36.770
but if you go out
of stock on coffee,

01:12:36.771 --> 01:12:38.570
you won't skip the diapers.

01:12:38.571 --> 01:12:40.560
Okay. So, that's actually

01:12:40.561 --> 01:12:41.900
a really important thing
because there's

01:12:41.901 --> 01:12:44.190
lots of stuff in the
data that's correlated,

01:12:44.191 --> 01:12:48.255
and a simple just
matrix factorization

01:12:48.256 --> 01:12:49.880
won't be able to tell if

01:12:49.881 --> 01:12:51.800
just coffee and diapers go

01:12:51.801 --> 01:12:55.350
together because you need
coffee to enjoy diapers,

01:12:55.351 --> 01:12:57.830
or if they go together because

01:12:57.831 --> 01:13:00.935
Sleepless people with babies
need a lot of coffee.

01:13:00.936 --> 01:13:02.860
So, those things you can't tell

01:13:02.861 --> 01:13:03.530
the difference and just

01:13:03.531 --> 01:13:04.655
the matrix
factorization approach.

01:13:04.656 --> 01:13:06.375
How could you possibly
tell the difference?

01:13:06.376 --> 01:13:09.765
Will you need data where
the diapers bought a stock,

01:13:09.766 --> 01:13:11.030
or the diapers price goes

01:13:11.031 --> 01:13:12.570
up or the diapers
price goes down?

01:13:12.571 --> 01:13:14.050
You actually have to measure

01:13:14.051 --> 01:13:15.840
whether when the price goes down,

01:13:15.841 --> 01:13:17.625
people respond in
the other product,

01:13:17.626 --> 01:13:20.890
and that's the key
identification strategy here.

01:13:20.891 --> 01:13:23.320
So, in this simulation,

01:13:23.321 --> 01:13:26.325
these up arrows show
we estimated a model,

01:13:26.326 --> 01:13:29.675
and then we predicted
what would happen if we

01:13:29.676 --> 01:13:31.230
predict onto a test set that has

01:13:31.231 --> 01:13:33.230
higher than usual
prices of coffee,

01:13:33.231 --> 01:13:36.550
and higher than usual
prices of taco shells.

01:13:36.551 --> 01:13:38.260
So, this is in

01:13:38.261 --> 01:13:39.920
the not-think-head model and

01:13:39.921 --> 01:13:41.250
this is in the think-ahead model.

01:13:41.251 --> 01:13:43.730
So, we see in
the not-think-ahead model,

01:13:43.731 --> 01:13:45.110
the Myopic Model, when you raise

01:13:45.111 --> 01:13:47.005
the prices of taco shells,

01:13:47.006 --> 01:13:50.010
you still predict that
people buy taco seasoning

01:13:50.011 --> 01:13:51.440
because they're going to go

01:13:51.441 --> 01:13:52.980
and look for the taco seasoning,

01:13:52.981 --> 01:13:55.065
but they're not going
to look think-ahead,

01:13:55.066 --> 01:13:57.550
that the taco shells
are too expensive,

01:13:57.551 --> 01:13:59.400
and so they don't want
the taco seasoning.

01:13:59.401 --> 01:14:00.945
But in the look-ahead model,

01:14:00.946 --> 01:14:03.054
when the taco shells
are expensive,

01:14:03.055 --> 01:14:04.770
the model predicts that people

01:14:04.771 --> 01:14:06.570
won't buy taco seasoning either,

01:14:06.571 --> 01:14:07.700
because they look ahead and

01:14:07.701 --> 01:14:08.940
they understand that they're not

01:14:08.941 --> 01:14:11.455
going to buy the taco
shells. So, yeah?

01:14:11.456 --> 01:14:14.290
&gt;&gt; So, I feel like this example
points out to me that

01:14:14.291 --> 01:14:16.120
the likelihood function
is going to be

01:14:16.121 --> 01:14:17.425
independence latent
order that you're

01:14:17.426 --> 01:14:19.210
not going to observe. Right.

01:14:19.211 --> 01:14:22.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so you would,
you infer it but it's,

01:14:22.781 --> 01:14:25.460
the behavioral model implies

01:14:25.461 --> 01:14:31.005
a probability distribution
over orderings,

01:14:31.006 --> 01:14:34.240
but if we had a different
behavioral model,

01:14:34.241 --> 01:14:36.295
we can't tell the difference

01:14:36.296 --> 01:14:39.135
between the different behavioral
models from the data,

01:14:39.136 --> 01:14:42.015
except for like by
broad goodness of fit measures.

01:14:42.016 --> 01:14:43.910
&gt;&gt; So, I guess I didn't,

01:14:43.911 --> 01:14:45.060
I still feel like
there's going to

01:14:45.061 --> 01:14:47.630
be touching identification
problems here.

01:14:47.631 --> 01:14:49.060
I can't tell the difference

01:14:49.061 --> 01:14:51.660
between one unobserved
ordering and

01:14:51.661 --> 01:14:52.500
whatnot observes that have

01:14:52.501 --> 01:14:53.780
preferences for different items

01:14:53.781 --> 01:14:55.965
are boring, and
a different observation.

01:14:55.966 --> 01:14:58.470
&gt;&gt; The price variation and out of

01:14:58.471 --> 01:15:01.530
stock variation sufficiently
should pin everything down,

01:15:01.531 --> 01:15:02.920
but I'm not going to
argue that I have

01:15:02.921 --> 01:15:06.145
that much price variation
in my data set.

01:15:06.146 --> 01:15:07.420
&gt;&gt; Going to the store where

01:15:07.421 --> 01:15:08.750
things actually are
geographically,

01:15:08.751 --> 01:15:10.845
and see if that lines
up with your line.

01:15:10.846 --> 01:15:12.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I mean, I
think if you really

01:15:12.691 --> 01:15:14.825
wanted to get into
that level of detail,

01:15:14.826 --> 01:15:17.700
it would be good to have
online browsing data

01:15:17.701 --> 01:15:19.140
so that you could
actually observe

01:15:19.141 --> 01:15:20.960
the consumers behavior.

01:15:20.961 --> 01:15:24.910
So, let me just show you
a couple of other quick things.

01:15:24.911 --> 01:15:28.215
So, here because
we published this,

01:15:28.216 --> 01:15:30.010
republishing the center between

01:15:30.011 --> 01:15:31.890
machine learning audience and
not to economics audience,

01:15:31.891 --> 01:15:34.010
we have a lot less
descriptive statistics.

01:15:34.011 --> 01:15:38.270
But here we show that
our model does well,

01:15:38.271 --> 01:15:40.940
and what we do, instead
of the other model

01:15:40.941 --> 01:15:42.080
where the other paper where we

01:15:42.081 --> 01:15:43.960
held out days with price changes,

01:15:43.961 --> 01:15:46.580
here we're just going
to look at subsets of

01:15:46.581 --> 01:15:49.260
test data that have
different prices,

01:15:49.261 --> 01:15:51.755
20 percent, 30 percent
different prices,

01:15:51.756 --> 01:15:53.170
and we show that our model of

01:15:53.171 --> 01:15:55.390
the relative performance
of our model it

01:15:55.391 --> 01:15:59.270
gets better when we get to
the more extreme prices,

01:15:59.271 --> 01:16:00.950
and these are different versions

01:16:00.951 --> 01:16:02.610
of models that
David Blei has used like

01:16:02.611 --> 01:16:05.060
hierarchical Poisson
factorization and

01:16:05.061 --> 01:16:07.515
various embedding models
that he's used from text.

01:16:07.516 --> 01:16:10.615
So, he's showing that relative
to David state of the art,

01:16:10.616 --> 01:16:12.460
machine learning embedding models

01:16:12.461 --> 01:16:14.950
incorporating
the price information in

01:16:14.951 --> 01:16:17.100
the complementarity
end does better

01:16:17.101 --> 01:16:20.345
when the prices are
different than average,

01:16:20.346 --> 01:16:22.360
and that's what you're hoping
because we want to do well

01:16:22.361 --> 01:16:24.805
predicting in unusual settings.

01:16:24.806 --> 01:16:28.070
So then, this is
what we discover.

01:16:28.071 --> 01:16:30.030
So again, I told the model now

01:16:30.031 --> 01:16:32.540
nothing about what the products
were, just the UPCs,

01:16:32.541 --> 01:16:35.400
and we do things like
we discovered cheese,

01:16:35.401 --> 01:16:37.285
and we discovered baking,

01:16:37.286 --> 01:16:39.250
and we discovered fruit,

01:16:39.251 --> 01:16:41.370
and we discovered toilet paper.

01:16:41.371 --> 01:16:44.780
So, that's lovely. So,
we can basically figure

01:16:44.781 --> 01:16:48.520
out what the products are as
well as what substitutes.

01:16:48.521 --> 01:16:51.780
So, just here, the last slide,

01:16:51.781 --> 01:16:53.320
I want to give some examples of

01:16:53.321 --> 01:16:54.930
the things you can do here.

01:16:54.931 --> 01:16:56.305
Here we're going
to query the data.

01:16:56.306 --> 01:16:59.550
So, if we query mission
tortillas soft tacos,

01:16:59.551 --> 01:17:02.200
we can see which items
are the most

01:17:02.201 --> 01:17:05.770
complimentary according to
our estimated user preferences

01:17:05.771 --> 01:17:07.505
with mission tortilla soft taco.

01:17:07.506 --> 01:17:09.475
We find taco shells,

01:17:09.476 --> 01:17:11.350
taco seasoning, and taco mix,

01:17:11.351 --> 01:17:14.010
taco seasoning mix are
those complimentary with

01:17:14.011 --> 01:17:17.300
top tacos, hot dog buns,

01:17:17.301 --> 01:17:20.685
yeah, they are also complimentary

01:17:20.686 --> 01:17:26.510
with these other hot dog buns

01:17:26.511 --> 01:17:28.310
which we then could think about,

01:17:28.311 --> 01:17:30.735
maybe there's
a taste for variety.

01:17:30.736 --> 01:17:34.375
Mustard is complementary with
hamburger buns and so on.

01:17:34.376 --> 01:17:35.730
So, I tell my kids
that I worked for

01:17:35.731 --> 01:17:37.420
three years, and
at the end of it,

01:17:37.421 --> 01:17:40.755
I discovered that they mustered
goes with hamburger buns.

01:17:40.756 --> 01:17:42.840
So, at some level
like this is not

01:17:42.841 --> 01:17:44.860
the deepest scientific discovery
you'd ever want to make.

01:17:44.861 --> 01:17:46.830
But the idea would be that if you

01:17:46.831 --> 01:17:48.800
think if you're Amazon or eBay,

01:17:48.801 --> 01:17:51.150
and you have these really
big product assortments,

01:17:51.151 --> 01:17:53.470
and you don't really
know what kinds of

01:17:53.471 --> 01:17:54.640
things compete with each other

01:17:54.641 --> 01:17:55.900
and what kinds of
things go together,

01:17:55.901 --> 01:17:57.240
this could be helpful.

01:17:57.241 --> 01:17:58.900
In particular, utilizing

01:17:58.901 --> 01:18:00.520
one particular time can
be a problem because,

01:18:00.521 --> 01:18:01.940
you give a sale on one product

01:18:01.941 --> 01:18:03.040
and it looks like
you're doing great,

01:18:03.041 --> 01:18:04.550
but you've actually
stolen the demand from

01:18:04.551 --> 01:18:06.350
another product or similarly,

01:18:06.351 --> 01:18:08.165
you have a sale on
one product and you're not,

01:18:08.166 --> 01:18:09.970
you're underestimating
the benefit of

01:18:09.971 --> 01:18:12.030
getting people to buy
more of another product.

01:18:12.031 --> 01:18:14.620
So, those types of
counterfactuals are interesting.

01:18:14.621 --> 01:18:17.160
So just to conclude, I would say,

01:18:17.161 --> 01:18:19.040
I hope I've communicated
effectively,

01:18:19.041 --> 01:18:21.345
that this is all a work
in progress.

01:18:21.346 --> 01:18:23.840
The goal I hope was clear that we

01:18:23.841 --> 01:18:26.035
want to be able to do
these kinds of counterfactuals.

01:18:26.036 --> 01:18:27.540
The challenges maybe are clear,

01:18:27.541 --> 01:18:29.800
that it's actually hard
to convince people,

01:18:29.801 --> 01:18:31.150
that you've really identified

01:18:31.151 --> 01:18:33.630
the structural parameters
through counterfactuals.

01:18:33.631 --> 01:18:36.020
That's not just because I'm Liem,

01:18:36.021 --> 01:18:38.630
it's because it's hard
to find data that

01:18:38.631 --> 01:18:41.425
actually has enough variation
to figure this stuff out.

01:18:41.426 --> 01:18:43.870
But if you have
big datasets and you

01:18:43.871 --> 01:18:46.150
do have price changes and
you do have out of stock,

01:18:46.151 --> 01:18:48.870
then you start to learn
enough to make this better.

01:18:48.871 --> 01:18:51.035
So, I would say
the bigger research agenda

01:18:51.036 --> 01:18:53.650
bringing together ideas from
reinforcement learning,

01:18:53.651 --> 01:18:55.180
in inverse
reinforcement learning,

01:18:55.181 --> 01:18:58.065
in matrix factorization
and econometrics,

01:18:58.066 --> 01:18:59.865
is to be able to help

01:18:59.866 --> 01:19:02.350
companies as well as
like customers of Azure,

01:19:02.351 --> 01:19:05.360
and others be able to
make these decisions.

01:19:10.950 --> 01:19:13.060
&gt;&gt; Your kids have been shocked

01:19:13.061 --> 01:19:14.140
to find that three years
[inaudible].

01:19:14.141 --> 01:19:17.845
All right.

01:19:17.846 --> 01:19:19.625
Okay, some questions. Yeah.

01:19:19.626 --> 01:19:24.310
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, did you do any
during holdout foundation.

01:19:24.311 --> 01:19:28.100
Are you would you trying to
validate on the back door?

01:19:28.101 --> 01:19:29.660
&gt;&gt; So, I think

01:19:29.661 --> 01:19:34.600
that if one thing I would
love to see change,

01:19:34.601 --> 01:19:36.630
is if the Machine
Learning audiences,

01:19:36.631 --> 01:19:38.850
I feel often don't have
a lot of patients for

01:19:38.851 --> 01:19:42.520
30 or 40 pages of
empirical analysis.

01:19:42.521 --> 01:19:45.025
But in the paper we have aimed
for the marketing journal,

01:19:45.026 --> 01:19:47.240
we spend more time trying

01:19:47.241 --> 01:19:50.090
to do these holdout sets
and really identify,

01:19:50.091 --> 01:19:55.710
here's days that we held
out data from consumers in

01:19:55.711 --> 01:19:58.650
weeks where there were
price changes and then we

01:19:58.651 --> 01:20:00.430
evaluated ourselves on how we fit

01:20:00.431 --> 01:20:02.500
for those consumers
on those weeks.

01:20:02.501 --> 01:20:06.500
So, yes. So, the goal
is to tune and

01:20:06.501 --> 01:20:11.180
evaluate yourself on held-out
data on weeks with changes.

01:20:11.181 --> 01:20:13.320
It's that, so normally

01:20:13.321 --> 01:20:15.765
the process would just
be hold out data,

01:20:15.766 --> 01:20:17.500
how do we predict.

01:20:17.501 --> 01:20:19.420
Is it any different than saying,

01:20:19.421 --> 01:20:21.620
"Well, now we have to hold out.

01:20:21.621 --> 01:20:25.095
Hold out experiments
or counterfactuals,

01:20:25.096 --> 01:20:26.920
or is it basically
just that simple,

01:20:26.921 --> 01:20:28.020
or there are more things that you

01:20:28.021 --> 01:20:29.600
consider when you
are trying to.".

01:20:29.601 --> 01:20:30.320
Well, so I think there's

01:20:30.321 --> 01:20:31.480
a couple of things
first of all you'd

01:20:31.481 --> 01:20:33.895
like to hold out
experiments, absolutely.

01:20:33.896 --> 01:20:36.315
In some sense, you want
the experiments to be

01:20:36.316 --> 01:20:38.575
representative of the ones
that you would do,

01:20:38.576 --> 01:20:41.435
and they need to be different
from what's in your data.

01:20:41.436 --> 01:20:44.250
But so, that's the first
differences and you

01:20:44.251 --> 01:20:46.905
want to make sure that
you're testing changes.

01:20:46.906 --> 01:20:48.765
The second is what's
your test statistic,

01:20:48.766 --> 01:20:50.940
because if my question is

01:20:50.941 --> 01:20:53.260
I want to raise the price
for all consumers,

01:20:53.261 --> 01:20:55.490
then my test statistic
should be something about

01:20:55.491 --> 01:20:59.170
predicting overall who bought
this product on this day.

01:20:59.171 --> 01:21:01.300
That's actually more
aggregated test statistic,

01:21:01.301 --> 01:21:02.380
and you actually make a different

01:21:02.381 --> 01:21:04.165
bias-variance trade off for that.

01:21:04.166 --> 01:21:05.600
Then if I said, "Oh,

01:21:05.601 --> 01:21:07.845
what I want to do is send
personalized coupons,

01:21:07.846 --> 01:21:10.050
then my test statistic
would be about

01:21:10.051 --> 01:21:12.890
individual responses
to price changes,

01:21:12.891 --> 01:21:13.810
and then you would make

01:21:13.811 --> 01:21:15.640
a different
bias-variance trade-off.

01:21:15.641 --> 01:21:17.010
So, one of the things
we weren't really

01:21:17.011 --> 01:21:18.275
thinking about going in,

01:21:18.276 --> 01:21:18.890
we were thinking about

01:21:18.891 --> 01:21:20.929
just prediction versus
counterfactual.

01:21:20.930 --> 01:21:22.660
Then we surprised ourselves,

01:21:22.661 --> 01:21:23.670
because it all let's just

01:21:23.671 --> 01:21:24.740
starts by tuning for prediction,

01:21:24.741 --> 01:21:25.950
it will be pretty similar,

01:21:25.951 --> 01:21:27.930
but it was actually
very different numbers of

01:21:27.931 --> 01:21:30.320
latent variables and so on
depending on whether you

01:21:30.321 --> 01:21:35.585
wanted an aggregate test
statistic or a more micro one.

01:21:35.586 --> 01:21:38.270
So, that's actually a little bit
unsatisfying though,

01:21:38.271 --> 01:21:39.900
because it says you have to

01:21:39.901 --> 01:21:41.440
know the question you're asking,

01:21:41.441 --> 01:21:42.530
answering to choose a model,

01:21:42.531 --> 01:21:45.045
and it's harder to make
a general purpose model.

01:21:45.046 --> 01:21:47.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I get it.

01:21:47.171 --> 01:21:48.300
&gt;&gt; Can you go through what

01:21:48.301 --> 01:21:49.670
this numbers are again
just because it looks

01:21:49.671 --> 01:21:51.460
like private brand hamburger buns

01:21:51.461 --> 01:21:52.605
are top three complements,

01:21:52.606 --> 01:21:54.185
and top three substitutes.

01:21:54.186 --> 01:21:57.860
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, this is
not substitutes,

01:21:57.861 --> 01:22:00.155
this is something we
call the exchangability.

01:22:00.156 --> 01:22:02.310
So, it turns out that it
was actually very hard to

01:22:02.311 --> 01:22:05.120
identify substitutes
in data like this,

01:22:05.121 --> 01:22:08.005
because basically most stuff
it's just never purchased.

01:22:08.006 --> 01:22:09.780
So, it's hard to tell
that two things,

01:22:09.781 --> 01:22:11.795
they're substitutes versus
you just never bought them.

01:22:11.796 --> 01:22:13.410
So, what we actually introduced

01:22:13.411 --> 01:22:14.720
a new concept which we call,

01:22:14.721 --> 01:22:17.020
I mean it's not exchangability
is on new concept,

01:22:17.021 --> 01:22:18.210
but as applied here,

01:22:18.211 --> 01:22:21.605
what we said two things are
exchangeable if they're

01:22:21.606 --> 01:22:22.980
into pairwise interactions with

01:22:22.981 --> 01:22:25.150
all other products are the same.

01:22:25.151 --> 01:22:27.465
There's two reasons things
could be exchangeable.

01:22:27.466 --> 01:22:29.195
If they're always
bought together,

01:22:29.196 --> 01:22:30.410
they would have the same pairwise

01:22:30.411 --> 01:22:31.660
interactions with everybody.

01:22:31.661 --> 01:22:33.450
If they are never
bought together,

01:22:33.451 --> 01:22:34.560
they would have the same pairwise

01:22:34.561 --> 01:22:35.810
interactions with everybody.

01:22:35.811 --> 01:22:38.685
So, then the idea is that if we

01:22:38.686 --> 01:22:41.860
take things that are

01:22:41.861 --> 01:22:44.819
exchangeable and they're
not compliments,

01:22:44.820 --> 01:22:46.265
maybe those are substitutes.

01:22:46.266 --> 01:22:48.240
Because just empirically,
we got a bunch of

01:22:48.241 --> 01:22:50.535
new ways trying to
identify substitutes.

01:22:50.536 --> 01:22:52.360
That gets back to
modelling the zeros.

01:22:52.361 --> 01:22:54.110
So, another part of
our research agenda

01:22:54.111 --> 01:22:55.470
is to have better modeling

01:22:55.471 --> 01:22:56.910
of the zeros which might help

01:22:56.911 --> 01:22:58.750
us hone in on substitutes
a little bit more.

01:22:58.751 --> 01:22:59.650
&gt;&gt; It might help.

01:22:59.651 --> 01:23:03.110
&gt;&gt; Yes, this remark
you made about

01:23:03.111 --> 01:23:05.915
basically different
matrix leading to

01:23:05.916 --> 01:23:09.490
different parameters
being selected was one.

01:23:09.491 --> 01:23:11.710
I guess when I saw that earlier,

01:23:11.711 --> 01:23:13.610
it made me a little bit
nervous in the sense

01:23:13.611 --> 01:23:16.785
that so you're lying
to some degree on,

01:23:16.786 --> 01:23:19.750
you're getting some reasonably

01:23:19.751 --> 01:23:21.160
good parameter identification

01:23:21.161 --> 01:23:22.340
to make the predictions right,

01:23:22.341 --> 01:23:24.100
you're not just relying

01:23:24.101 --> 01:23:28.370
on having good predictive
accuracy from the model.

01:23:28.680 --> 01:23:31.410
To me if the answer

01:23:31.411 --> 01:23:33.530
is changing based on
the question you're asking,

01:23:33.531 --> 01:23:35.770
that to some degree
revealing that there

01:23:35.771 --> 01:23:38.230
is non-identifiability going
on with the parameters,

01:23:38.231 --> 01:23:40.990
and so you're you're
not learning quite

01:23:40.991 --> 01:23:44.900
the best ones you
can from the data.

01:23:44.901 --> 01:23:47.400
&gt;&gt; Let me speak that. That's
a deep, deep question.

01:23:47.401 --> 01:23:49.015
So, I think in economics,

01:23:49.016 --> 01:23:51.310
this is probably why I think
people are going to have

01:23:51.311 --> 01:23:54.665
some mixed reactions to this
or they are generally more,

01:23:54.666 --> 01:23:55.985
I thought people might hate it.

01:23:55.986 --> 01:23:57.700
In economics, they
actually liking it better

01:23:57.701 --> 01:23:59.410
than I thought, but there's some.

01:23:59.411 --> 01:24:00.620
It's a real weighted
different way

01:24:00.621 --> 01:24:01.470
of thinking about things.

01:24:01.471 --> 01:24:04.340
We used to think there is
one true structural preference,

01:24:04.341 --> 01:24:05.430
and I'm going to learn it,

01:24:05.431 --> 01:24:06.320
and my theorem has to be

01:24:06.321 --> 01:24:07.420
when angle to infinity,
I'll learn it,

01:24:07.421 --> 01:24:10.170
I nail it, I get
precise confidence intervals,

01:24:10.171 --> 01:24:11.645
I know your preferences.

01:24:11.646 --> 01:24:13.175
Some level like that just here,

01:24:13.176 --> 01:24:15.380
I mean how can I
really know what you

01:24:15.381 --> 01:24:16.450
feel about lettuce when I saw

01:24:16.451 --> 01:24:17.720
you buy lettuce three times.

01:24:17.721 --> 01:24:19.320
So, I think there is a benefit

01:24:19.321 --> 01:24:20.530
to the Machine
Learning perspective,

01:24:20.531 --> 01:24:22.325
which is it's all
an approximation,

01:24:22.326 --> 01:24:24.865
and I just want to have
a better approximation.

01:24:24.866 --> 01:24:26.510
So, that is a slightly

01:24:26.511 --> 01:24:28.705
more pragmatic
engineering approach,

01:24:28.706 --> 01:24:30.560
but you can only
operationalize it if you have

01:24:30.561 --> 01:24:32.920
a test set that

01:24:32.921 --> 01:24:34.890
says how well do I do
it what I care about,

01:24:34.891 --> 01:24:36.330
which then in some sense requires

01:24:36.331 --> 01:24:37.655
you to have held out experiments.

01:24:37.656 --> 01:24:39.760
Because if I can't, if
exactly as you say,

01:24:39.761 --> 01:24:42.830
there are many models that
would equally fit the data.

01:24:42.831 --> 01:24:44.330
It's not they're not identified

01:24:44.331 --> 01:24:45.510
whether you have
10 latent factors,

01:24:45.511 --> 01:24:46.600
or 50, or 70.

01:24:46.601 --> 01:24:48.485
I don't know which one it is,

01:24:48.486 --> 01:24:51.105
but I want to know what's
the best approximation

01:24:51.106 --> 01:24:52.960
to your preferences for

01:24:52.961 --> 01:24:54.950
the purpose of doing a
certain counter-factual.

01:24:54.951 --> 01:24:56.870
But it's very unappealing that

01:24:56.871 --> 01:24:59.045
you have a different model
for different questions.

